26-only row check, an exit-code-only pull check, and the three-check inventory counter-example: verification scope must span every dimension the operation could change | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/a-guard-that-cannot-run-fails-open]] | The dead Windows dcg hook and the never-fired Perch/VALD alerts both look identical to success; protective mechanisms need a liveness signal and a deliberate periodic trip | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/decommissioned-leg-verified-by-skipped-markers]] | Retired steps left in `refresh_all_data.bat` as greppable `skipped` markers make a cutover checkable by expected line count (3); the still-registered 8 PM no-op task is the residual reporting hazard | 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/deferred-credential-rotation-recorded-rationale]] | Exposed VALD client secret deliberately not rotated (private repo, pulls work) and recorded as \"skip for now\"; a defensible deferral missing the falsifiable revisit trigger its sibling had | 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/write-the-decision-where-each-consumer-will-look]] | One handoff plus four per-repo `STATUS.md` records: placement follows how the next reader arrives at the question, and deferrals need the record more than completed work does | 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/repo-behind-blocks-nightly-push]] | `repo-behind-blocked:global-memory` failed two nights because the backup never auto-merges; pull then a clean 21:12 run, plus persistent-vs-transient push failure triage | 2026-09-24.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/mac-evening-machine-schedule-split]] | Mac evening-machine role reaches the schedulers: rituals disabled (persists across reboot), backup 09:00→21:00 with `.bak-2026-09-24`, no-installer check before the hand edit, CRASHBOX prerequisite moved | 2026-09-24.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/sync-commits-mask-most-recent-work]] | Adds the 2026-09-29 commit-days ranking, which fixed the automation noise but under-weighted new work (`Flint` absent from 26 active), showing commit metrics cannot name the intended thread | 2026-09-19.md, 2026-09-24.md, 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[connections/check-for-a-generator-before-hand-editing]] | Asking who writes a config file decides the shape of the fix; the absence of a generator is what makes a hand edit durable, the inverse of fix-the-generator | 2026-09-24.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/false-alarms-are-fixed-at-the-check-not-the-threshold]] | Chronic alarms get fixed at the check or by re-siting the work; threshold tuning is legitimate only after one of those changed what the alarm means. | adjudicated | 2026-09-27 |\n| [[connections/the-second-reviewer-is-a-frame-not-an-extra-pass]] | A second review adds value in proportion to how differently framed it is, and relying on reviewer outages to supply that frame makes it arrive at random. | adjudicated | 2026-09-27 |\n| [[concepts/hook-command-quoting-home-expansion]] | Single-quoted `$HOME` in a hook command blocks expansion → exit 127 surfaced as a \"non-blocking status\" error; startup \"HOOK FAILURES\" lines are a different signal (scheduled jobs) | 2026-09-16.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/stale-git-lock-files-block-mass-pull]] | An interrupted overnight run left 138 empty `.git/*.lock` files silently blocking all pulls; moved not deleted as `*.lock.stale-2026091x` | 2026-09-16.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/dev-folder-is-not-a-document-store]] | `Claude outputs` backup failure resolved by moving two postgame `.docx` to Drive per the season README rather than widening the manifest; Drive tree reads OOM-killed | 2026-09-16.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[connections/one-artifact-two-storage-regimes-pick-one]] | `Claude outputs` (decided, moved) and session transcripts (undecided, growing) show that extending coverage from the wrong home is cheap once and expensive forever | 2026-09-16.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/git-fetch-before-declaring-clean]] | `/dev-sync` called ~140 repos clean without ever fetching; fetching revealed 5 behind — recorded as the skill's single biggest failure mode | 2026-09-19.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/daily-backup-push-error-miscount]] | `PUSH ERR \\| Everything up-to-date` — git's no-op success message counted as an error, plus every log line written twice | 2026-09-19.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/never-trim-a-memory-line-without-checking-for-a-second-copy]] | A compress attempt at 2,496/2,500 chars nearly destroyed a fact's only copy; move to the Tier A slate and replicate cross-machine instead | 2026-09-19.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/concurrent-claude-sessions-same-repos]] | Two Claude sessions on the same repos: bad-grep-looks-like-revert, per-session handoffs, a redundant `Linda_Projects` clone, real `global-memory` conflicts | 2026-09-19.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[connections/a-checks-silence-is-not-a-negative-result]] | An un-fetched clean verdict and a mis-quoted grep both return reassuring silence because the check could not speak; capability belongs in the output | 2026-09-19.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge]] | Both machines added the same `Flint` router line to `RESEARCH.md`; a router index is a set keyed by identity, so the correct merge is deduplicate — not union, not newest-wins | 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[concepts/blind-answer-key-before-first-api-call]] | Jev triage pilot gated on writing and committing Claude's blind answer key before any API call; pre-registration standing in for an absent reviewer, with the sample pinning the population | 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[concepts/committed-sample-enables-cross-machine-testing]] | Flint's books/OCR/audio are Windows-only, but a committed 200-file `sample.csv` plus a mounted Drive made the triage leg Mac-runnable; credentials remain per-machine (untested API key) | 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[concepts/carried-forward-maintenance-residue-2026-09-29]] | `SecondBrain` outside `/dev-sync`'s scope, `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom` recurring since 9/24, a 9/27 flush failing on a session limit, and a located-but-unopened `perch-pull` log | 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[connections/classify-the-conflict-by-the-artifacts-kind]] | Four cross-machine conflicts, four correct resolutions — discard, union, merge-by-key, deduplicate — each set by what the file is, so no blanket rule is safe | 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n\n\n## Related Wiki Articles\n\n### concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\"\naliases: [union merge transcripts, TOS_Evidence_Brief missing from manifest, 146 repos walked, Linda Projects space breaks sync step, Tier A does not cross machines, backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22, 22 active 117 dormant, manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom, delete only the conflict markers, keep both sides no from-files, RESEARCH.md duplicate entry]\ntags: [git, sync, backup, macos, memory, workflow, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-16.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-22.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-24.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-29.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-22\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\n\nA `/dev-sync` run on 2026-09-22 walked 146 repositories to bring the Mac in line with GitHub: two pulled (`_INBOX`, `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`), one pushed (`CommandCentre`), one cloned (`TOS_Evidence_Brief`, created on Windows 9/21), with 22 active and 117 dormant-and-clean, and no conflicts. Its durable findings concern how recurring cross-machine friction is resolved: a merge strategy for append-only logs, a manifest that does not discover its own new inputs, and the fact that Tier A memory does not travel between machines. The transcript-merge finding was already established on 2026-09-16 and recurred again on 2026-09-24, making it a three-instance pattern; the manifest gap recurred on 9/24 and again on 9/29.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Union merge is the right strategy for append-only transcript logs.** Mac/Windows conflicts in `global-memory/transcripts/*.md` resolve cleanly by keeping every line from both machines, producing no `.from-*` files to clean up afterward.\n- **The same resolution was reached independently on 2026-09-16**, where `global-memory` transcript-log conflicts were settled by keeping both sides rather than picking a winner, again with no `.from-*` files created — before the merge strategy was configured.\n- **The manual form is to delete only the conflict markers.** On 2026-09-24 two clashing transcript logs (9/23 and 9/24) were resolved exactly this way — text from both sides kept, only the `<<<<<<<`/`=======`/`>>>>>>>` lines removed — confirming that for this artifact class there is nothing to adjudicate.\n- **The union strategy is class-specific, not general.** On 2026-09-29 a conflict on the router file `~/DEV/RESEARCH.md` was a *duplicate* `Flint` entry added independently by both machines, and the correct resolution was to drop one line — a union would have listed the project twice.\n- **The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos.** `TOS_Evidence_Brief` — created on Windows the previous day — was absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it fixed the report (prior file at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`). On 2026-09-24 the identical class reappeared as `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom`, and the same entry was still outstanding on 2026-09-29.\n- **Tier A memory does not cross machines.** Durable GPS decisions were therefore written into the repo's own `docs/STATUS.md` *and* `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so the Windows machine would see them.\n- **A folder name containing a space (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke a sync step again** and had to be redone with the path quoted — a recurrence of the same hazard seen on 2026-09-11.\n- **`compile.py` was deliberately not run.** A compile costs ~$1.28, so clearing the memory-compiler backlog was left as the user's call; the warning persists in `.errors.log` until then, and had grown to 3 logs behind by 2026-09-24.\n\n## Details\n\nThe union-merge finding names a resolution strategy that fits the artifact's semantics rather than a generic \"take one side\" rule. A transcript log is append-only and each machine appends independently, so the two versions are not competing revisions of the same content — they are disjoint additions to a shared record. Under that structure the correct merge keeps every line, and the usual conflict-file machinery (`.from-*` copies awaiting manual adjudication) is pure overhead because there is nothing to adjudicate. The pattern now has three independent confirmations arriving by different routes: a hand resolution on 9/16 that consciously declined to pick a winner, a configured merge driver on 9/22, and a purely mechanical marker-strip on 9/24. That an unaided human judgment, a configured strategy, and a mechanical cleanup all converge on identical output is the strongest available evidence that the strategy matches the artifact rather than the tooling — and it argues that the merge driver should have been configured after the first instance rather than the second. This is the same classify-by-kind-before-resolving discipline applied in [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]], where treating accumulating records as revisions would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows, and the accumulate-versus-supersede distinction generalized in [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]]. Notably, the 9/22 PFF loader bug was the identical error one layer down — a replace where a merge was required.\n\nThe 2026-09-29 `RESEARCH.md` conflict is the boundary case that keeps the union strategy honest. Both machines had independently added a router entry for the new `Flint` project, so the conflict was concurrent agreement rather than disagreement — and the correct resolution was to keep one line and drop the other, because a router index is a set keyed by project identity rather than an append-only sequence. Applying the transcript strategy there would have produced a doubled entry. The practical rule, developed in [[concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge]] and synthesised across all four observed artifact classes in [[connections/classify-the-conflict-by-the-artifacts-kind]], is that a merge strategy is correct only for the class it was derived from, and that a conflict on the `~/DEV` router files should be read as an expected duplicate rather than investigated as a mangled file.\n\nThe manifest gap is a recurrence rather than a novelty, and its recurrence is the point. A repo created on one machine does not announce itself to a backup manifest maintained on another; the manifest's coverage is declared, not discovered, so a new repo is invisible to it until a human adds a line. The 09:05 backup error was in this case an honest signal — the manifest check did fire — but the general hazard is the one catalogued in [[connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success]] and [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]]: every managed item succeeds while the unmanaged item is never counted. Prior instances at [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] and [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]], the `Claude outputs` complaint resolved on 9/16 by relocation rather than by a manifest entry ([[concepts/dev-folder-is-not-a-document-store]]), the `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom` entry two days after this run, and that same entry still outstanding a further five days later ([[concepts/carried-forward-maintenance-residue-2026-09-29]]), put the count well past the threshold at which — by the reasoning in [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — the fix belongs in the discovery mechanism rather than in yet another manual manifest edit. Each individual fix is a one-line change, which is precisely why the structural fix keeps getting deferred.\n\nThe Tier A observation is a routing constraint with immediate practical force. Tier A memory is local to the machine that wrote it, so any decision that a second machine must honor — here, that the Mac owns the pulls and the Windows GPS writer is stopped — cannot live there alone. Writing it into the repository's `docs/STATUS.md` puts it in a location git already replicates, and mirroring it into `global-memory/MEMORY.md` reaches the synced memory surface; between them the decision arrives on the other machine by two independent paths. This extends the content-to-tier routing matrix in [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] with a cross-machine axis, and matches the working-machine-authors-the-fix pattern in [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]]. Finally, the deliberate refusal to run `compile.py` is a recorded non-action rather than an oversight: a ~$1.28 spend is a user decision, so the backlog warning was left standing in `.errors.log` with the command documented in the compiler's README — consistent with treating `.errors.log` as an escalation channel that clears only on a verified clean run.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge]] — The 2026-09-29 router conflict showing that the union strategy is class-specific.\n- [[connections/classify-the-conflict-by-the-artifacts-kind]] — The four-class taxonomy this run's union merge anchors.\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The prior conflict triage that established classifying by kind before resolving; union merge is the resolution for the accumulating-record kind.\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] — Why an append-only log must be merged rather than overwritten.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — An earlier instance of the same declared-not-discovered manifest gap.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed manifest guard designed against exactly this omission.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-is-not-a-document-store]] — The 2026-09-16 manifest complaint resolved by relocating the folder instead of widening coverage.\n- [[concepts/carried-forward-maintenance-residue-2026-09-29]] — The same manifest entry and compiler backlog still outstanding a week later.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: an unlisted item cannot fail a check it was never part of.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — The 2026-09-11 sync where `Linda Projects`' embedded space first broke the parallel fetch loop.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The tier-routing matrix this session extends with a cross-machine constraint.\n- [[concepts/compiler-backlog-hook-notice]] — The backlog warning deliberately left in `.errors.log`, at 3 logs behind by 2026-09-24.\n- [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] — Why the warning stays until a verified clean compile, not until the fix is merely intended.\n- [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] — The GPS decisions whose cross-machine visibility motivated the dual write.\n- [[concepts/mac-evening-machine-schedule-split]] — The 2026-09-24 session in which both the union-merge and manifest-gap findings recurred.\n- [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — The recurrence argument for moving the manifest fix into discovery.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-16.md]] — End-of-day wrap session (backfilled 2026-09-19 12:24): `global-memory` transcript-log conflicts resolved by keeping both sides rather than picking a winner, with no `.from-*` files created — the first of three instances of the union-merge resolution.\n- [[daily/2026-09-22.md]] — Sync and closeout session (17:25): `/dev-sync` walking 146 repos with `_INBOX` and `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` pulled, `CommandCentre` pushed, `TOS_Evidence_Brief` cloned, 22 active / 117 dormant / no conflicts; union merge resolving Mac/Windows transcript-log conflicts without `.from-*` files; `TOS_Evidence_Brief` missing from `backup-manifest.json` causing the 09:05 backup error, fixed with a `.bak-2026-09-22` backup; `DEV/Linda Projects` space breaking a sync step; Tier A memory not crossing machines, resolved by writing to `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md`; `compile.py` (~$1.28) deliberately left as the user's call.\n- [[daily/2026-09-24.md]] — Evening KeystoneOS closeout (21:25): two clashing transcript logs (9/23, 9/24) resolved by keeping text from both sides and deleting only the conflict markers; `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom` reported by the backup log and queued as an action item; memory compiler 3 logs behind with a manual `compile.py` run still outstanding.\n- [[daily/2026-09-29.md]] — Memory maintenance session (08:46): router conflict on `RESEARCH.md` where both machines had added a `Flint` entry, resolved by hand by dropping the Mac's duplicate line with no `.from-*` files created and recorded as the expected shape of a `RESEARCH.md` merge; `RunningTheRoom` still missing from the daily-backup manifest, recurring since 9/24; memory compiler still backlogged a few logs.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/memory-cap-relief-by-migration-not-deletion.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Memory Cap Relief by Tier Migration, Not Deletion\"\naliases: [MEMORY.md 101 percent, 2500-byte cap, doctor warns at 95 percent, migrate facts to Tier A, project_dormant-threads.md, wordsmithing buys bytes migration buys headroom, re-run the doctor to verify, memfile-full, memory writes take effect next session, three cap fixes in one day]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, maintenance, automation, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-04.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-07.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-04\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# Memory Cap Relief by Tier Migration, Not Deletion\n\nOn 2026-09-04 at 07:38, a `/claude-stack-doctor` run found `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md` at 101% of its 2,500-byte cap. The file was brought to 94% not by deleting facts but by relocating them into Tier A project memory, on the explicit principle that no fact should be lost to a size limit. The session also established the empirical proportion between the two available remedies: trimming prose recovers a few bytes, while migrating content recovers real headroom. The identical fix was performed again on 2026-09-07 at 18:13 under the same warning (`memfile-full:MEMORY.md`), with the same three relocations and the same conclusion — and then a *third* time the same evening at 21:30, trimming 2,531 → 2,496 characters by removing four lines whose detail had just been written into Tier A. A `/claude-stack-doctor` run at 18:05 had reported the file at 85% (2,133/2,500), so the file crossed from comfortable to over-cap twice within a single evening.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The doctor warns at 95% of the 2,500-byte cap** and reports the condition as `memfile-full:MEMORY.md`; the file was found at 101%, meaning the next write would have failed outright rather than degrading gracefully.\n- **Every over-cap fact was migrated, none deleted.** Tezino CampPrep and FaceComps simulation details went to a new `~/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/project_dormant-threads.md`; a Cowork \"upload a zip\" note was appended to `project_personal-site-build-routes.md`.\n- **One item was removed as a genuine duplicate,** not as a size concession: a CLI-lessons \"how to resume\" note already existed verbatim in `project_terminal-fluency-workspace.md`.\n- **Prose trimming alone did not clear the cap.** The file only dropped below threshold after actual facts were relocated — wordsmithing buys a few bytes, migration buys headroom. Restated on 2026-09-07 as: consolidating a Tier B file means re-homing content, not rephrasing it.\n- **The cap is re-approached on a timescale of hours, not weeks.** Three separate relief operations on 2026-09-07 (18:13 and 21:30, against an 18:05 reading of 85%) show the file's working volume sitting at the ceiling, so writing new Tier B facts should be paired with routing older ones down.\n- **Verification was done by re-running the doctor**, requiring both \"ALL CLEAR\" and exit code 0, rather than assuming the edit had been sufficient — consistent with the standing rule that a clean exit alone is not evidence.\n- **Edits were left uncommitted deliberately,** for the 09:00 `dailybackup` launchd job to pick up. The trimmed file takes effect next session, since the memory snapshot is frozen mid-session — so the fix cannot be confirmed in the session that made it.\n\n## Details\n\nThe migration-over-deletion principle deserves stating as policy rather than as a one-time choice. A character cap on a working-memory file is a constraint on what is *resident in every session's context*, not a constraint on what may be known — the tiered architecture exists precisely so that content can be demoted from always-loaded to retrievable-on-demand. Deleting a fact to satisfy the cap conflates the two and destroys information to solve a budgeting problem. Every relocation performed here preserved the fact at a path the relevant project session will load, so the only thing actually reduced was the standing per-session cost. This is the same lever described in [[connections/keep-it-retrievable-not-resident]] and applied earlier to the Tier A index trim in [[concepts/system-health-check-remediation]], now exercised one tier down.\n\nThe measured proportion between remedies is the reusable finding. Faced with a file a few percent over cap, the tempting first move is compression — shorter sentences, dropped articles, abbreviations — because it requires no decisions about where content belongs. This session establishes that the move is nearly worthless at this scale: rewording bought a handful of bytes and left the file still over the threshold, and only relocating whole facts moved it to 94%. The corollary is that cap pressure should be read as a routing signal rather than a writing-style signal — the file is over because it holds content that belongs elsewhere, and the correct response is to ask which tier each item is addressed to. The earlier stack-doctor sweep in [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] reached the same file at 100% and resolved it by shortening wording while keeping all 16 threads; that this file returned to over-cap three times on a single later day confirms that the wording route defers the problem rather than solving it.\n\nThe 2026-09-07 evening also reframes cap relief from an incident to a routine. Two independent sessions hours apart both had to trim the same file, and the 21:30 trim followed directly from that session having written a new Tier A memory file — the relocation and the trim were two halves of one operation, since the four removed lines were removed *because* their detail now lived one tier down. Read that way, the correct discipline is not \"fix the cap when the doctor complains\" but \"when you write a Tier A fact, check whether its Tier B summary line can now shrink.\" That makes cap pressure a byproduct of normal writing rather than a periodic emergency, and it explains why a file at 85% early in an evening can be over by the end of it.\n\nTwo procedural notes generalize beyond this file. The verification is performed by re-running the diagnostic and requiring the \"ALL CLEAR\" string and exit 0, rather than inferring success from the byte count after the edit — the same discipline of checking the authoritative reporter rather than the intermediate measurement. And the *timing* of the effect matters: the memory snapshot is read at session start and frozen, so a trim made mid-session cannot be validated by observing the current session's behavior. The correct confirmation step is deferred to the following session, which means an over-cap condition fixed today is only demonstrably fixed tomorrow. During the 2026-09-07 passes a shell guard also blocked writes whose paths contained a variable or targeted the home directory — twice in one day — forcing a fallback to the native file tools, a specific instance of the pattern in [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]], with the operative rule being to use literal absolute paths and the Write tool for memory writes.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The Tier A/B/C structure that makes migration possible; the routing matrix there is what decides which project file each relocated fact belongs in.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The health check that surfaced the over-cap condition and was re-run to verify the fix.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — The same file at 100% three weeks earlier, resolved by shortening wording rather than migrating; the recurrence is evidence that wording buys less durable relief.\n- [[concepts/system-health-check-remediation]] — The Tier A index trim (15 → 10 files) applying the same standing-context-reduction lever one tier up.\n- [[concepts/tier-a-memory-as-the-durable-payload-of-a-verification-session]] — The 21:30 session whose Tier A write made the third trim of the day possible; relocation and trim as one operation.\n- [[concepts/canonical-section-relocation-inbound-pointers]] — The same evening's relocation of the tool-path table, done for canonicalization rather than under cap pressure.\n- [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]] — The variable-path and home-directory writes blocked during these sessions, resolved with the native file tools.\n- [[concepts/git-sync-status-versus-uncommitted-work]] — Why leaving the trimmed file uncommitted for the 09:00 job leaves a window in which the sync indicator reads green over uncommitted work.\n- [[connections/keep-it-retrievable-not-resident]] — The general principle: cut what is always loaded, never what is retrievable.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-04.md]] — Stack-doctor session (07:38): `MEMORY.md` found at 101% of its 2,500-byte cap with the doctor warning threshold at 95%, migration of Tezino CampPrep/FaceComps details to a new `project_dormant-threads.md`, Cowork note appended to `project_personal-site-build-routes.md`, duplicate CLI-lessons note removed, 101% → 94% achieved only after fact relocation, verification by re-running the doctor for \"ALL CLEAR\" and exit code 0, edits left uncommitted for the 09:00 `dailybackup` job, and all other checks healthy.\n- [[daily/2026-09-07.md]] — Stack-doctor session (18:13): the same 101% over-cap condition diagnosed as `memfile-full:MEMORY.md`, the same three relocations performed, \"wording compression alone was not enough\" restated as re-homing rather than rephrasing, a shell guard blocking a variable-containing write path, verification by re-running the script (ALL CLEAR, exit 0), and the note that memory writes take effect next session rather than the current one. An earlier run the same evening (18:05) had reported the file at 85% (2,133/2,500). Closeout session (21:30): a third trim, 2,531 → 2,496 chars, removing four lines whose detail had just been written into a new Tier A file, plus a second same-day shell-guard block on a home-directory redirect.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Stack Doctor Sweep (2026-08-11): Self-Inflicted Repo Divergence and a Lying Dry Run\"\naliases: [claude-stack-doctor sweep, repo-diverged skills, skill-tax false positive, daily-backup.ps1 dry-run bug, MEMORY.md 100 percent, SecondBrain two-writer conflict, flush-error version skew 0.2.136]\ntags: [claude-code, automation, maintenance, git, memory, powershell, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-01\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Stack Doctor Sweep (2026-08-11): Self-Inflicted Repo Divergence and a Lying Dry Run\n\nA `/claude-stack-doctor` run on 2026-08-11 after a three-day gap took the automation/memory stack from 8 issues to 6 (three of the remaining six being lagging indicators that clear on the next nightly backup). The sweep's most valuable findings were not the fixes themselves but three cases where the *monitoring layer* was the defect: a nightly backup that committed its own generated files and so diverged a repo every night, a `skill-tax` check measuring the wrong quantity, and a `-DryRun` flag that reported git operations it never performed.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **`repo-diverged:skills` was self-inflicted.** Six tracked files were regenerated every run (`last-weekly-run.log` plus five `__pycache__` entries), so the nightly backup committed churn and diverged the repo nightly. Untracking and gitignoring them was the sweep's highest-leverage fix.\n- **`daily-backup.ps1 -DryRun` was lying.** The `Log` calls at lines 28/38 sat *outside* the `if (-not $DryRun)` guards at 24/34, so a dry run printed \"committed / pushed\" for git operations it never ran — briefly fooling the sweep itself. Fixed to `[dry-run] would commit`.\n- **`skill-tax` was a false positive from the monitor's own logic:** it counted all 133 on-disk `SKILL.md` files with no knowledge of `skillOverrides`. Verified empirically first (0 of 72 overridden skills leak into the listing; 61 expected, 60 seen), *then* the script was patched — the two candidate fixes were opposite, so the discriminating check had to precede either.\n- **`flush-error` recurrence across 8/08 and 8/11 indicated version skew, not contention:** `claude-agent-sdk` was 26 versions behind (0.2.110 → 0.2.136); upgrading restored flushes, and the memsearch flush patch was confirmed to survive the package operation.\n- **Both sides preserved on a two-writer conflict.** Four SecondBrain morning briefings differed from committed origin versions; origin's won by default and are live, with local copies staged at `doctor-backup-20260807-135924/secondbrain-local-briefings/*.local.md` — fully reversible rather than adjudicated on the spot.\n\n## Details\n\nThe three monitoring-layer defects share a structure worth naming: each produced a confident, well-formatted signal about a system state it was not actually measuring. The dry-run bug is the purest form — logging placed outside the guard it describes means the flag's entire purpose (previewing without acting) is silently void, and the operator's trust in the preview is inversely related to its accuracy. `skill-tax` is the same failure one level up: the check counted files on disk, a proxy that was correct before `skillOverrides` existed and became wrong afterward, so a previously-completed cleanup appeared not to have landed. The correct response in that case was explicitly *not* to fix either candidate immediately, because the two possible remedies (clean up more skills, or patch the counter) pointed in opposite directions; running the discriminating check first — do overridden skills leak into the listing? — decided which. The `repo-diverged:skills` case is the automation eating its own output: a backup job that commits generated artifacts guarantees divergence on every run, making a real alarm fire nightly for a cause the alarm cannot describe.\n\nThe sweep's restraint is as instructive as its fixes. Several issues were deliberately left alone with reasons recorded: 73 unversioned skill files (a design fork — adding `~/.agents/skills` to `backup-manifest.json` was proposed but not applied), unmanaged `DEV` directories (the user's scope call), a 4-log compiler backlog (self-healing at 2/night against ~1/day, and forcing it costs real money), and `snapshot:sessions` (an rsync race against a live Codex file, not rot). Two items were escalated as genuinely not auto-fixable and left open: Outlook auth expired, so morning briefings were shipping with 0 email threads pending an interactive `reauth.sh` run, and Google Drive 154h stale, with files reaching the local `G:` cache but GoogleDriveFS not syncing. The `MEMORY.md` file was at 100% — the next write would have failed — and was consolidated to 93% by shortening wording while keeping all 16 threads, rather than dropping any. Finally, two `flush-error` session IDs were classified benign only after evidence (the IDs exist nowhere; the DEV slug holds 28 transcripts, 3 in the last 24h), on the explicit principle of not classifying by inference; and two self-corrections were made before reporting (an \"8 → 2\" claim against a raw verdict of 6, and a claim that the /doctor changes were unversioned when the nightly backup had in fact committed them).\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The recurring health-check routine this sweep instantiates; that article's post-restart ALL CLEAR is the same tool run under different conditions.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — The `claude-agent-sdk` version-skew failure mode that recurred here (0.2.110 → 0.2.136), matching the documented `uv`-upgrade remedy.\n- [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]] — The `~/.claude/skills` `git pull` that destroyed 73 files on 8/07; this sweep verified 21/21 symlinks resolve and 154 `SKILL.md` reachable after its own pull.\n- [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] — The skill-listing budget that `skill-tax` exists to monitor; the false positive was a measurement error against that same ceiling.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — Another case where a backup subsystem's status reporting was narrower or staler than the operational question being asked.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The general pattern the dry-run and `skill-tax` findings both instantiate.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — Stack doctor sweep (backfilled 2026-08-29 15:02): 8 → 6 verdict, `repo-diverged:skills` traced to six self-generated tracked files, `skill-tax` false positive verified empirically before patching, `daily-backup.ps1 -DryRun` logging outside its guards, `claude-agent-sdk` 26-version skew, `MEMORY.md` 100% → 93% with all 16 threads intact, SecondBrain two-writer conflict resolved by staging both sides, deliberately-deferred items with reasons, two open user-actionable items (Outlook reauth, GoogleDriveFS), and two pre-report self-corrections. Handoff at `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-08-11-stack-doctor-sweep.md`.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Distinguishing Stale from Live Entries Before Clearing an Error Log\"\naliases: [.errors.log archival, stale error entries, error log clearing procedure, archive then clear, escalation log hygiene, live vs stale escalation]\ntags: [automation, maintenance, memory, macos, workflow, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-31.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-31\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Distinguishing Stale from Live Entries Before Clearing an Error Log\n\nThe `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` file is the asynchronous escalation channel through which scheduled jobs surface failures to the next interactive session. Because it is append-only and read at session start, it accumulates entries from failures that have since been fixed, and a stack-doctor pass must decide per-entry whether each one still describes a live condition. On 2026-08-31 the log's entries were confirmed stale, then archived and cleared per the skill's documented procedure.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The error log is an escalation channel, not a state record.** Scheduled tasks write to it because no session exists at run time; entries persist until manually cleared regardless of whether the underlying failure was resolved.\n- **Clearing requires a staleness determination first.** Each entry must be checked against current ground truth — the relevant job's log, the artifact it was supposed to produce — before the log is emptied, or a live failure is silently discarded.\n- **Archive, then clear.** The procedure preserves the entries elsewhere rather than deleting them outright, keeping the failure history available while restoring the log's signal value for the next session.\n- **An uncleared log degrades into noise.** Because it is surfaced at every session start, stale entries train the reader to skim past it, which is the same alarm-fatigue dynamic that motivated raising the memsearch session-end alarm threshold from 3 to 6.\n\n## Details\n\nThe escalation log exists to solve a specific structural problem: a scheduled job that fails at 06:15 or 09:00 has no session to report to, so it writes to a durable file that the next session start reads. That design makes the log write-only from the job's perspective — nothing in the pipeline knows when a failure has been remediated, so nothing removes the entry. The log therefore represents \"failures that were reported at some point,\" not \"failures that are currently true,\" and any maintenance pass over it is fundamentally a reconciliation task rather than a cleanup task.\n\nThe reconciliation is per-entry and requires an independent source. On 2026-08-31 the determination was made by checking the backup log's timeline: the entries corresponded to the 09:00 manifest failure, and the 10:31 re-run had already completed clean, so every entry described a resolved condition. Had any entry lacked such a corroborating success, clearing the log would have erased the only surviving record of an unresolved failure. Archiving before clearing hedges against a wrong staleness call — the entries remain recoverable if a later session discovers the condition was live after all — and it also preserves the failure history for pattern analysis across maintenance passes.\n\nThe cost of not doing this is not data loss but signal loss. A log that is displayed at every session start and reliably contains only old, already-fixed entries stops being read, at which point the escalation channel it implements has failed silently while appearing to work. Keeping it empty except when something is genuinely wrong is what makes its non-emptiness informative.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — Defines the stack-doctor routine and the `.errors.log` escalation mechanism that scheduled tasks write to; this article covers the maintenance of that log.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The same 2026-08-31 session; the manifest failure was the source of the entries that were confirmed stale and cleared.\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — The alarm-threshold raise (3→6) that addressed the same noise-versus-signal problem from the opposite direction: making an alarm fire less often so that firing means something.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — Explains why scheduled jobs need an asynchronous escalation file at all: they run without a session to report to.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-31.md]] — Stack-doctor session (10:34): `.errors.log` entries confirmed stale rather than live, archived and cleared per the skill's documented procedure.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Harness-Agnostic AIOS Plan (Mac + Windows Skills/Backup Stack)\"\naliases: [harness-agnostic-aios, 2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md, AIOS plan, skills repo cross-platform plan, 42 own skills 19 third-party, Task 2 prune 306 to 208, claudex-loop codex-review out of scope]\ntags: [keystoneos, claude-code, skills, cross-platform, planning, git, macos, windows]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-12.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-12\nupdated: 2026-09-13\n---\n\n# Harness-Agnostic AIOS Plan (Mac + Windows Skills/Backup Stack)\n\nThe \"harness-agnostic AIOS\" plan (`~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md`, pushed as `00730b6`) is a multi-task effort to make the skills and backup stack function identically on both the Mac and the Windows machine. Tasks 1–2 were completed on 2026-09-12; the plan itself remained unapproved at round 10 of its review gate, having accumulated a scope error, a backup-breaking safety hook, and several smaller defects that review surfaced before implementation.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The ownership inventory was wrong until review corrected it:** of 19 outward symlinks in the skills repo, 14 are third-party package-manager installs (including 5 `mattpocock-skills` explicitly out of scope). The corrected framing is **42 skills Eric wrote, 19 third-party links**.\n- **Task 4's inversion script was deleted entirely** rather than fixed — copying third-party vendored links into a personal repo was the wrong operation, not a buggy one. The four vendored links (`claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review`) stay out of `claude-skills` under the same rule that excludes plugins; Windows gets a separate install of the providing package.\n- **Task 2's prune took the global skills pool 306 → 208**, with 61 skills still resolving and roughly 150 third-party installs untouched. It was executed as a **move, not a delete**, keeping the operation reversible.\n- **Round-10 findings, still open:** a serious one (the new safety hook silently breaks the daily backup — see below), plus wording that still said `.gitignore` where `.git/info/exclude` is correct, and a Pi test user likely inheriting the `staff` group and stalling a boundary step.\n- **The dead symlinks were already committed and pushed** before this session; the decision converts a silent Windows breakage into an explicitly documented one with a stated remedy.\n\n## Details\n\nThe plan's central defect was a scope error rather than a logic error, and it is the kind that only surfaces when someone asks who owns each file. A skills repository that mixes hand-authored skills with package-manager-installed ones presents a uniform surface — every entry is a symlink, and every symlink looks equally like a candidate for a \"make this work on Windows too\" transformation. Task 4 would therefore have copied 14 third-party installs into a personal repository, mixing vendor-managed content into a tree whose whole value is that its contents are authored and maintained locally. The corrected inventory (42 own, 19 third-party) is the durable artifact here, and it should be re-derived rather than remembered, since the vendored count moves whenever a package is installed or dropped — the same reasoning that made the backup job count unsafe to memorize in [[connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number]].\n\nThe decision to exclude the four vendored links extends an existing rule rather than inventing one. Plugins are already excluded from the skills repo because they are installed, not authored; the vendored symlinks are the same category wearing a different shape. The consequence — those four skills are dead links on Windows until the providing package is installed there — is accepted deliberately, which matters because the links had already been committed and pushed and were *already* dead on Windows. The change is from an undocumented breakage to a documented one with a named remedy, which is the same disposition applied to `DexaProject`'s permanent manifest warning in [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]]: an accepted condition recorded with its reason is not a defect.\n\nTask 2's prune demonstrates the reversibility discipline that recurs throughout this stack. Moving 98 entries out of the global pool rather than deleting them means an incorrect prune costs a move back rather than a restore from backup, and it sits alongside the tarball snapshots of [[concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline]] as the same instinct applied at a lower stakes level. The residual 61-resolving / ~150-untouched split is also the number to verify after any future prune, since it is the ratio rather than the absolute that describes a healthy pool.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]] — The exclusion mechanism this plan depends on, and the trailing-slash defect that would have silently re-added the dead links.\n- [[concepts/backup-verification-by-restore]] — The restore test run against this plan's backup, which exposed a two-hop symlink chain into an unbacked-up vendor folder.\n- [[concepts/cross-provider-review-gate]] — The review gate that caught the Task 4 scope error after a provider switch.\n- [[concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern]] — The `~/.agents/skills` → Claude → Codex symlink topology whose vendored half this plan excludes.\n- [[concepts/skills-repo-git-hygiene]] — Prior hygiene work in the same repo, including the explicit-path staging discipline that keeps unowned content out of commits.\n- [[concepts/skills-repo-worktree-branch-cleanup]] — The preceding cleanup pass on the same repo and its `dcg` permission gate.\n- [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]] — The `mv`-with-variable-paths block hit during Task 2, worked around with Python containment checks.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-12.md]] — Memory maintenance session (17:31): plan `00730b6` pushed, Tasks 1–2 complete, reviewer's discovery that 14 of 19 outward symlinks are third-party, inventory reframed as 42 own / 19 third-party, Task 4 inversion script deleted, four vendored links excluded with a separate Windows install as the remedy, Task 2 prune executed as a move (306 → 208 global pool, 61 resolving, ~150 third-party untouched), and three round-10 findings left open.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-04.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Outlook Inbox Triage Action List (2026-06-04 Run)\"\naliases: [2026-06-04 inbox triage, Catapult renewal overdue 6-04, Playfly timecard overdue 6-04, needs-reply follow-ups 6-04, Joe Wargo Aptive 8 days stale, Nat Shattock Jun 17 presentation]\ntags: [productivity, workflow, email, communication, triage, baylor-athletics, inbox]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-04.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-04\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# Outlook Inbox Triage Action List (2026-06-04 Run)\n\nOn 2026-06-04, a morning Outlook inbox triage produced a prioritized summary of pending communications categorized into needs-reply, follow-ups, meetings to schedule, forms owed, still-open, and FYI buckets. The run is an instance of the recurring [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]] and a near-continuation of the prior two days' triages; its two highest-urgency items were again both overdue obligations carried forward — a Catapult Sports renewal (4 days past its 5/31 due date) and a Playfly LLC timecard (4+ days overdue).\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Two overdue obligations as top priority:** the Catapult Sports renewal (due 5/31, now 4 days past) and the Playfly LLC timecard verification in isolved (due 5/30, 4+ days overdue) were both flagged for immediate response — the age × importance heuristic again surfacing compliance/vendor items whose consequences escalate daily.\n- **Distribution:** 7 needs-reply, 2 stale follow-ups, 2 meetings to schedule, 3 forms owed, 2 still-open threads, and 40+ FYI/newsletter items.\n- **Needs-reply items:** Nat Shattock (Jun 17 Baylor campus presentation agenda, 1 day old), Luke Bogus (Dropback two-paths-forward direction, 3 days old), Lauren Green (Baylor Volleyball onboarding/password reset, 6 days old), and Brandon Miller (career inquiry, 3 days old).\n- **Stale follow-ups:** Austin Lewis (1080 Sprint Demo confirmation, 6 days old) and Joe Wargo (Aptive Index Review meeting, now 8 days old — carried from the 7-day flag on the 6-03 run).\n- **Meetings and forms:** Laurel Proulx + Carrie (Female Athlete Pelvic Floor survey planning call) and Baylor Athletics Performance Clinic staff attendance (Courteney Tompkins) to schedule; AMHS Education summer feedback survey and PFF translations (due 2026-07-01) among forms owed; a GitHub Claude app permissions request also flagged for review.\n\n## Details\n\nThe 2026-06-04 triage is a near-continuation of the [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-03]] and [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-02]] runs: the Catapult renewal, Playfly timecard, 1080 Sprint demo (Austin Lewis), Joe Wargo Aptive Index, Brandon Miller career inquiry, Lauren Green onboarding, and Luke Bogus Dropback threads all recur, with overdue counters incremented (Joe Wargo 7→8 days stale, Catapult now 4 days past). This day-over-day persistence again demonstrates the methodology's core value: a categorized snapshot makes accumulating communication debt visible, and the age-based sort within each category keeps escalating obligations from being masked by the day's 40+ FYI/newsletter volume. The Catapult renewal is another manifestation of the recurring Catapult OpenField vendor friction across Baylor projects. The following day's [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-05]] run carries these same threads forward with counters again incremented.\n\nThe triage was the first of several sessions that day; later sessions pivoted to OKR staff-meeting rollout prep (see [[concepts/okr-review-facilitation-framework]] and [[concepts/applied-performance-okr-system]]) and the Fall Camp macro load model design (see [[concepts/baylor-fall-camp-macro-load-model]]). A memory flush at 14:21 recorded a FLUSH_ERROR (command exit code 1), an instance of the intermittent memory-compiler flush failures documented elsewhere.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]] — The structured 6-category audit methodology this run instantiates; the needs-reply/follow-ups/meetings/forms bucketing and age × importance prioritization applied here are defined there.\n- [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-03]] — The prior-day run sharing nearly all of this run's threads; comparing the two shows overdue counters incrementing day over day.\n- [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-05]] — The following-day run continuing the same thread set with counters again incremented.\n- [[connections/catapult-token-friction-across-baylor-projects]] — The Catapult renewal item is another instance of recurring Catapult OpenField vendor friction across Baylor projects.\n- [[concepts/okr-review-facilitation-framework]] — The OKR staff-meeting rollout prep built later the same day.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-04.md]] — Inbox triage session (08:14): 7 needs-reply, 2 stale follow-ups, 2 meetings to schedule, 3 forms owed, 2 still-open, 40+ FYI; Catapult renewal (4 days overdue) and Playfly timecard (4+ days overdue) flagged OVERDUE; Joe Wargo Aptive 8 days stale, PFF translations due 7/1, GitHub Claude app permissions request flagged.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/system-health-check-remediation.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code System Health Check and Remediation Session\"\naliases: [system health check, memsearch timeout raise, memory injection audit, Tier A index trimming, memsearch Milvus healthy, auto-mode blocks .claude edits, claude-agent-sdk upgrade, errors log cleanup, MEMORY.md index trim]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, tooling, maintenance, memsearch, milvus, infrastructure, configuration]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-25.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-01\nupdated: 2026-08-01\n---\n\n# Claude Code System Health Check and Remediation Session\n\nOn 2026-06-25, a system health-check and remediation session worked through four flagged maintenance items — memsearch/Milvus timeout tuning, memory-compiler SDK upgrade, calendar auth, and a memory-injection audit — plus a cleanup of the resolved-error noise in `.errors.log`. The session raised memsearch's timeout thresholds, upgraded `claude-agent-sdk`, trimmed the Tier A auto-injection index from 15 to 10 files for token savings, and cleared an overdue pending decision from global memory.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **memsearch timeout raised, two layers in sync:** the internal timeout in `memsearch-sync.js` was raised 25s→45s, and the `settings.json` hook's outer limit was raised 28s→50s to match. The outer hook limit must exceed the internal timeout or the hook is killed before memsearch can complete.\n- **claude-agent-sdk upgraded 0.2.101→0.2.110** in `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler` to fix flush-failed errors — a continuation of the version-skew maintenance pattern (see [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]]).\n- **Tier A auto-injection index trimmed 15→10 files:** four files (Zotero CLI, AI Integration Initiative, Athlete Data Governance, Sport Science Symposium) were removed from auto-injection but kept on disk (still memsearch-searchable). Estimated token savings ~8,800→~6,700 injected per session.\n- **Milvus was healthy the whole time:** the ETIMEDOUT errors were a memsearch spawn/timeout issue, not a Milvus outage — the obvious culprit (the vector DB) was not the cause. Milvus ran healthy in Docker throughout.\n- **Overdue pending decision cleared:** the stale \"memory injection cost audit\" pending decision was removed from global MEMORY.md and replaced with an OKR Q1 gate review dated 8/31; `.errors.log` was cleared of resolved ETIMEDOUT noise from 6/12–6/24.\n\n## Details\n\nThe session illustrates a recurring maintenance discipline: periodically working through flagged health-check items in a single dedicated pass rather than letting them accumulate. The memsearch timeout tuning addressed the ETIMEDOUT noise that had been accumulating in `.errors.log` since mid-June (see [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] for the concurrent-contention ETIMEDOUT pattern). Critically, the fix required adjusting two coordinated limits — the internal memsearch timeout and the outer hook timeout in `settings.json` — keeping the outer limit strictly larger so the hook does not terminate memsearch mid-operation. The diagnostic lesson was to not assume the obvious culprit: Milvus, the vector database memsearch depends on, was healthy in Docker the entire time; the timeouts originated in memsearch's own spawn/timeout behavior.\n\nThe Tier A index trimming is a token-efficiency operation on the memory-injection layer. The global MEMORY.md indexes Tier A project files for auto-injection at session start; as that index grows, each session pays a larger fixed token cost. Trimming the index from 15 to 10 files (removing four lower-priority project files from auto-injection while leaving them on disk) reduced the estimated per-session injection cost from ~8,800 to ~6,700 tokens. Because the removed files remain on disk and memsearch-searchable, no information is lost — only the always-on injection footprint is reduced, following the same Tier A/Tier B capacity-management logic documented in [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]]. A notable operational constraint surfaced during the session: the auto-mode classifier blocks edits to files under `.claude/` (including `settings.json` and per-project MEMORY.md files) even when those files are personal notes rather than active configuration. Such edits require an explicit user \"go ahead,\" and some operations (like `Clear-Content` on `.errors.log`) must be run manually by the user because the classifier will not permit the agent to perform them.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — The memsearch tool whose timeout thresholds were tuned here; the ETIMEDOUT errors cleared from `.errors.log` are the same concurrent-contention pattern documented there, now addressed by raising the timeout ceilings.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — The memory compiler whose `claude-agent-sdk` was upgraded 0.2.101→0.2.110 to fix flush-failed errors, continuing the version-skew maintenance pattern established there.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The three-tier memory architecture whose Tier A auto-injection index was trimmed for token savings; removed files stay memsearch-searchable, consistent with the Tier A cross-project retrievability property.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — The broader Claude Code environment maintenance discipline; this session is another instance of periodically triaging flagged health-check items before they degrade session quality.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-25.md]] — System health-check session (13:29): memsearch timeout raise (25→45s internal, 28→50s hook), claude-agent-sdk upgrade (0.2.101→0.2.110), Tier A index trim 15→10 files, overdue pending decision cleared and replaced with OKR Q1 gate review (8/31), `.errors.log` cleared, Milvus-healthy diagnostic lesson, auto-mode classifier `.claude/` edit-blocking behavior.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/scratch-folder-artifact-durability.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Scratch-Folder Artifacts Die With the Job\"\naliases: [job scratch folder trap, stale scratch paths in wrappers, graphify-out scripts relocation, durable path handoff, disposable folder artifacts, build_payloads.py]\ntags: [workflow, filesystem, automation, session-continuity, tooling, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-30.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-01.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-01\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Scratch-Folder Artifacts Die With the Job\n\nDuring the 2026-09-01 `~/DEV` graph refresh, fifteen operational scripts and two Codex verification reports were found living inside a disposable job scratch folder — a location that is removed when the job ends, taking the artifacts with it. All were relocated to durable paths under `~/DEV/graphify-out/` (scripts to `scripts/`, reports alongside the graph output), and the shell wrappers that invoked them were repointed because they still held stale scratch paths. The daily log notes this was \"a trap already flagged in user's notes,\" making it a repeat occurrence rather than a first discovery — and the 2026-08-30 coach-report prototype session had hit the same trap two days earlier.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Scratch folders are defined by the system as not surviving the job.** Anything whose value outlives the run — scripts, verification reports, resume instructions — must be written elsewhere at creation time, not rescued afterward.\n- **Relocation is incomplete without repointing the callers.** The shell wrappers still referenced the old scratch paths after the scripts moved; a move that leaves stale references produces a broken toolchain rather than a durable one.\n- **Handoff documents must reference durable paths.** The Codex reports were copied out of scratch specifically so the handoff would point at locations that still exist when the handoff is read.\n- **This is a recurring trap across unrelated projects.** On 2026-08-30 the coach-report prototypes' build scripts — notably `build_payloads.py`, which keeps opponent names and dates out of the markup — were found in the job's scratch folder and flagged for relocation into `~/DEV/Football/CoachReport_Prototypes/`. Its recurrence indicates the failure is structural, so the remedy is a default write location, not vigilance.\n- **The same sessions that corrected the trap reproduced it in their own handoffs:** `/var/folders/gw/.../T/handoff-baylor-coach-report-2026-08-30.md` on 8-30 and `handoff-arms-graph-2026-08-31.md` on 9-01, both temp paths.\n\n## Details\n\nThe mechanism is identical to the `/tmp` handoff loss documented in [[concepts/handoff-document-placement-discipline]], generalized from one document type to a whole class of artifacts. A scratch or temp directory is attractive at write time because it is unconditionally writable and requires no decision about where a file belongs; the cost is deferred entirely to the future moment when someone needs the file and it is gone. Scripts are a particularly bad fit for scratch because they are, by definition, the things you will want to run *again* — a fifteen-script toolchain in a disposable folder is a toolchain with a scheduled expiry, and a payload builder like `build_payloads.py` encodes a data-hygiene decision (keeping opponent names and dates out of the markup) that would have to be rediscovered if lost. The correct default is to write anything invocable or referenceable into the project tree next to the work it operates on, which is exactly where both relocations landed: `~/DEV/graphify-out/scripts/` and `~/DEV/Football/CoachReport_Prototypes/`.\n\nThe wrapper-repointing detail is the part most easily missed. Moving files to durable storage changes where they live but not what refers to them, and the shell wrappers had hardcoded scratch paths that survived the move as dangling references. A half-completed relocation is arguably worse than no relocation, because the artifacts now appear safe while the entry points into them are broken — the failure surfaces later, at invocation time, rather than at move time. The general rule is that durability work must sweep the reference graph, not just the files: after moving an artifact, grep for the old path and update every caller before considering the move done.\n\nThat two sessions two days apart both corrected this trap and reproduced it — relocating scripts out of scratch while writing their own handoffs into `/var/folders/.../T/` — is the strongest evidence that the fix cannot be attention. Each location was chosen by different reasoning at a different moment, and the temp path won every time nothing forced the question. A durable default (a project-local `handoffs/` or the project tree itself) removes the decision entirely, in the same way that deriving a backup manifest from a directory scan removes the omission failure mode in [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]].\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/handoff-document-placement-discipline]] — The same failure for handoff documents specifically, where `/tmp` placement made a file both reboot-fragile and invisible to a home-directory search.\n- [[concepts/dev-knowledge-graph-refresh-2026]] — The session where the fifteen scripts and two Codex reports were relocated and the wrappers repointed.\n- [[concepts/coach-report-prototype-flag-rule]] — The 2026-08-30 prototype session whose `build_payloads.py` and sibling build scripts hit the same trap.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — The execution-side analog: the process does not survive the session, just as the scratch artifact does not survive the job.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — Another artifact-durability gap in the same stack, and the same structural remedy of removing the human decision point.\n- [[connections/install-time-capture-goes-stale-when-host-moves]] — Stale hardcoded paths surviving a relocation is the same class of frozen reference.\n- [[connections/the-default-is-the-real-decision]] — Why a durable default beats a remembered rule: the choice kept only in human memory expires.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-30.md]] — Coach report prototype session (11:11): \"Build scripts lived in the job's scratch folder and die with the job — `build_payloads.py` (keeps opponent names and dates out of the markup) is the one worth preserving\"; action item to move it into `~/DEV/Football/CoachReport_Prototypes/`; the session's own handoff written to a `/var/folders/.../T/` path.\n- [[daily/2026-09-01.md]] — Graph refresh session (20:31): fifteen scripts relocated from the job scratch folder to `~/DEV/graphify-out/scripts/`, shell wrappers found holding stale scratch paths and repointed, both Codex verification reports copied into `~/DEV/graphify-out/` so the handoff points at durable paths, and the handoff itself written to a `/var/folders/.../T/` temp path.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/automation-stack-doctor.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Automation Stack Doctor and Self-Maintaining Scheduled-Task Cycle\"\naliases: [stack doctor, KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck, post-restart health check, self-deleting scheduled task, nightly self-maintaining cycle, daily-backup.ps1, backup execution limit, cloud-check UTC bug, stack doctor ALL CLEAR]\ntags: [claude-code, automation, windows, task-scheduler, memory, infrastructure, maintenance, scheduling, powershell]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-07-27.md\"\ncreated: 2026-07-27\nupdated: 2026-08-02\n---\n\n# Automation Stack Doctor and Self-Maintaining Scheduled-Task Cycle\n\nThe automation stack doctor is a health-check routine that verifies the full local Claude Code automation and memory stack — scheduled tasks, Python venvs, memsearch, Docker/Milvus, the error log, backups, and the Google Drive API heartbeat — and reports a consolidated status. On 2026-07-27, following a Windows reboot, a post-restart stack doctor run returned ALL CLEAR, and the session restructured the stack into a self-maintaining nightly cycle: heavy reindexing was moved into the 6:15 AM backup task while the session-end hook stays a light 45-second incremental pass.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Stack doctor scope:** a single run validates all 4 scheduled tasks, Python venvs, memsearch, Docker/Milvus survival across restart, error-log cleanliness, backup freshness, and a Google Drive API heartbeat. The 2026-07-27 post-restart run returned ALL CLEAR (backups fresh at 3h, Milvus survived reboot).\n- **Self-deleting post-restart check:** a one-shot scheduled task (`KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck`, triggered at logon + 5 min) auto-runs stack doctor after a reboot, escalates any failures to `.errors.log`, and deletes itself. This is the workaround for the fact that a session cannot literally wait through a machine restart — failures surface at the next session start via the error log.\n- **Nightly reindex folded into the 6:15 AM backup task** (`daily-backup.ps1`): uncapped, uses the full `memsearch.exe` path (per the Task Scheduler PATH lesson), escalates per-path failures, and resets the hook timeout-streak counter on success. Rationale: a first-time SecondBrain book-library index is an hours-scale job that a 45-second session-end hook can never absorb.\n- **Backup task execution limit raised 30 min → 2 h** so a heavy reindex night is not killed mid-pass. The session-end hook stays light (45 s incremental) and its alarm threshold was raised 3 → 6 so it only alarms if the nightly safety net itself falls behind — a real signal, not noise.\n- **Cloud-check timezone bug fixed:** a \"-4h old\" false backup-freshness reading came from timestamps not normalized to UTC after `ConvertFrom-Json`; the check now normalizes to UTC regardless of the return type.\n\n## Details\n\nThe session's central move is an architectural relocation of expensive work off the interactive/hook path and onto a scheduled job. The recurring memsearch indexing alarm had been misdiagnosed the prior day as an \"occasional\" hiccup; on 2026-07-27 it was recognized as structural — a first-time embedding job measured in hours (one indexer had burned 81 CPU-minutes and was still running). Because a 45-second session-end hook can never complete an hours-scale index, the correct home for that work is the nightly 6:15 AM `daily-backup.ps1` task, which runs uncapped and escalates per-path failures. The session-end hook is left as a light incremental pass, and its alarm threshold is raised from 3 to 6 consecutive misses so it only fires when the nightly safety net itself falls behind. With the library index converging over a night or two while search stays live, the alarm becomes a meaningful signal rather than chronic noise.\n\nTwo supporting reliability fixes accompanied the restructure. The backup task's execution time limit was raised from 30 minutes to 2 hours so a heavy reindex night is not terminated mid-pass — a scheduled job that runs an unbounded index needs an execution ceiling wide enough to absorb the worst case. The cloud-check UTC normalization fix addressed a false \"-4h old\" reading: after `ConvertFrom-Json`, timestamps were being compared without normalizing to UTC, so a local-vs-UTC offset was misread as staleness; normalizing regardless of the parsed return type eliminated the false alarm. The `KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck` self-deleting task closes the post-reboot verification gap — because no session can survive a restart, a one-shot logon-triggered task that runs stack doctor and escalates to `.errors.log` is the mechanism by which post-restart health reaches the user at the next session start. All changes were committed and pushed to the dotclaude and skills repos with no pending user action; the first fully self-maintaining cycle (backup → snapshot → reindex → alarm reset) was scheduled to run the following morning at 6:15 AM.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — The memsearch tool whose hours-scale first-index drove this restructure; the nightly reindex-in-backup-task architecture and the raised 3→6 alarm threshold are documented there as well.\n- [[concepts/windows-task-scheduler-path-config]] — The full-`memsearch.exe`-path requirement applied to the nightly reindex task is a direct application of this Task Scheduler minimal-PATH lesson.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — The architectural rationale for moving hours-scale reindexing out of the session entirely: long-running background commands do not survive a session, so scheduled jobs are their correct home.\n- [[concepts/system-health-check-remediation]] — An earlier single-pass health-check-and-remediation session on the same stack; the stack doctor generalizes that periodic triage discipline into an automated, scheduled routine.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-07-27.md]] — Post-restart stack doctor session (16:40): ALL CLEAR result, `KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck` self-deleting logon task registered, nightly reindex moved into 6:15 AM `daily-backup.ps1`, session-end hook left at 45 s with alarm threshold raised 3→6, backup execution limit raised 30 min→2 h, cloud-check UTC normalization bug fixed, PR #16 merged (`59b6f2c`).\n\n```\n\n### concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"dcg Guard False Positives and the Native-Tool Workaround\"\naliases: [dcg guard false positive, dcg blocks inline PowerShell, write script to file then run, compound Bash command blocked, split into separate calls, home-directory redirect block, native file tools as default route]\ntags: [claude-code, tooling, guard, powershell, bash, gotcha, workflow, macos]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-04.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-07.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-04\nupdated: 2026-09-12\n---\n\n# dcg Guard False Positives and the Native-Tool Workaround\n\nThe `dcg` command guard repeatedly blocks operations that are benign in context, producing a recurring class of false positive. The established response is to route around the shell entirely using the native file tools, which the guard evaluates differently. By 2026-09-07 the recurrence rate had promoted the native tools from fallback to default route; 2026-09-11 added two further blocked shapes — inline PowerShell invoked through Bash, and a compound Bash command — each resolved by decomposing the request rather than by arguing with the guard.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The guard reads the command's shape, not its effect.** Blocks have landed on home-directory redirects, inline PowerShell passed through Bash, and compound commands joining multiple operations — none of which are dangerous by virtue of their form.\n- **The reliable workarounds are decompositions, not escalations.** Write the inline script to a file and execute the file; split a compound command into separate calls; use the native Read/Write/Edit tools instead of shell redirection.\n- **Escalating the permission mode is the wrong rung for a false positive.** The block is not a correct refusal awaiting authorisation — it is a misclassification, and the narrowest sufficient response is to restate the operation in a shape the guard parses correctly.\n- **Frequency, not severity, justified the routing change.** Enough recurrences made the native file tools the default rather than the fallback, so the guard is simply not consulted for ordinary file work.\n- **New blocked shapes keep appearing**, which argues for treating \"the guard refused\" as a prompt to change form, not as evidence that the operation is problematic.\n\n## Details\n\nThe guard's failure mode is that it classifies on syntactic surface. A redirect whose target resolves under the home directory, a `powershell -Command \"...\"` string embedded in a Bash invocation, and an `&&`-joined pair of commands all share the property of being harder to parse into a single unambiguous intent — and the guard resolves that ambiguity conservatively. That is a defensible default for a guard, but it means the refusals carry no information about whether the underlying action is safe. The consequence for workflow is that the correct reading of a block is \"restate this more simply,\" and the three recorded workarounds are all instances of that single move: reduce the command to the simplest shape that expresses one operation.\n\nThe promotion of the native file tools from workaround to default route is the durable outcome. When a guard misfires often enough on one category of work, the efficient response is not to keep discovering the workaround per-incident but to stop routing that category through the guarded path at all. File reads, writes, and edits have a native tool surface that does not require constructing a shell command, so the guard's shape-based classification never enters the picture. This is a narrower and more targeted response than relaxing the guard would be — the guard continues to cover genuine shell operations, and only the category with a demonstrated false-positive rate is rerouted. It parallels the reasoning behind resolving blocked actions at the narrowest sufficient rung: the choice among a one-off approval, a mode switch, and a standing rule is decided by how often the situation recurs, and here the recurrence rate selected a structural reroute over repeated per-incident handling.\n\nThe inline-PowerShell case deserves separate note because its workaround — write the script to a file, then run the file — has a side benefit beyond satisfying the guard. A script on disk is inspectable, re-runnable, and diffable, whereas an inline string is consumed once and leaves no artifact. On a Mac where PowerShell is the established fallback for tasks the Bash tool handles unreliably, having those fallback scripts materialise as files rather than as one-shot strings makes the fallback path auditable in a way the guard was not trying to achieve but nonetheless produced.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-permission-modes]] — The permission layer above the guard; a false positive is not a case for escalating the mode.\n- [[connections/narrowest-sufficient-escalation-over-blanket-bypass]] — Frequency rather than risk selecting the response rung; here it selected a structural reroute.\n- [[connections/windows-bash-tool-no-output-powershell-fallback]] — The PowerShell fallback path whose inline form the guard blocks, resolved by writing the script to a file.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — The 2026-09-11 session where a compound Bash command was blocked and splitting it into separate calls worked.\n- [[concepts/system-health-check-remediation]] — The earlier auto-mode behaviour of blocking edits under `.claude/` even for benign personal files.\n- [[connections/preconditions-that-check-the-wrong-thing]] — A guard coupled to a property other than the one that matters.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-04.md]] — Initial false-positive shapes and the native-file-tool workaround.\n- [[daily/2026-09-07.md]] — Same-day recurrence promoting the native file tools from fallback to default route; home-directory redirect block recorded as a fourth shape.\n- [[daily/2026-09-11.md]] — Memory maintenance session (13:10): dcg blocks inline PowerShell invoked via Bash, with the workaround being to write the script to a file and run it. Sync session (21:41): a safety guard blocked a compound Bash command; splitting it into separate calls worked.\n\n```\n\n### connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Fix the Generator, Not the Artifacts It Produced\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts\"\n  - \"concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm\"\n  - \"concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-11\nupdated: 2026-09-12\n---\n\n# Connection: Fix the Generator, Not the Artifacts It Produced\n\n## The Connection\n\nThree findings in this stack share an identical structure: a tool emitted a stream of wrong outputs, the outputs were numerous and visible, and the correct fix was one change to the tool rather than N changes to the outputs. On 2026-09-11, the Windows `/dev-sync` save command was mangling encoding and prepending BOMs, producing roughly 200 phantom conflict files — the fix was to the save command, with most of the 200 left in place as harmless. The same day, `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` was recurring because the manifest check lacked symlink awareness — the fix was to teach the check about symlinks, not to add `_DRIVE` to the manifest. On 2026-08-11, `repo-diverged:skills` fired every night because the nightly backup was committing six files it regenerated on every run — the fix was to untrack and gitignore them, not to resolve the divergence nightly.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nIn each case there is a tempting per-artifact remedy that is both more obvious and strictly worse: delete the 200 conflict files, add `_DRIVE` to the manifest, resolve the divergence each morning. Each would clear the current symptoms and guarantee their return, because none of them touches the thing producing the symptoms. The diagnostic that distinguishes the two fixes is *recurrence*: a one-off wrong output is an artifact problem, but a wrong output that regenerates on a schedule is a generator problem, and the schedule is the tell. All three cases had that tell — nightly divergence, a manifest error appearing at every session start, conflict files accumulating with every sync — and in all three the count of artifacts was large enough to make the per-artifact fix feel like the serious response when it was in fact the one that would need repeating forever.\n\nThe second-order insight is about what the artifacts cost while they persist. They are not merely untidy; they degrade the channel that reported them. A `manifest-unmanaged` list that always contains `_DRIVE` trains the operator to skim a channel that also carries real coverage gaps — and the same 2026-09-11 run surfaced two genuine omissions, `KnowledgeManagement` and `SignalPlays`, alongside the permanent noise entry. A `repo-diverged:skills` alarm that fires nightly for a self-inflicted cause makes the next genuine divergence unremarkable. Fixing the generator is therefore not only cheaper in the long run; it is what restores the meaning of the signal. The exception that proves the rule is `DexaProject`, whose manifest warning is also permanent but is kept deliberately with a recorded reason — a documented exception carries information, whereas an unfixed false positive carries only noise.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-09-11 (13:10): ~200 `.from-*` conflict files traced to the Windows `/dev-sync` save command mangling encoding (`ΓÇö`) and prepending a BOM; the Windows side was fixed rather than only cleaning up symptoms, and 17 harmless leftovers plus 95 BOM-bearing files across 36 repos were deliberately left alone.\n- 2026-09-11 (13:10): `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` recurring at session start resolved by teaching the backup to skip symlinks — the check's predicate was corrected rather than an exception added — while two real gaps found by the same check were added to the manifest with a 0 lost / 0 changed / 2 added proof.\n- 2026-08-11: `repo-diverged:skills` firing nightly because `daily-backup.ps1` committed six files it regenerated each run (`last-weekly-run.log` plus five `__pycache__` entries); untracking and gitignoring them was recorded as the sweep's highest-leverage fix.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]]\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm]]\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]]\n- [[connections/the-monitor-is-the-defect]] — The adjacent case where the monitor itself is wrong; here the monitor is right about a world the generator keeps corrupting.\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — Why a channel carrying permanent benign entries stops functioning as an escalation path.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/model-agnostic-repo-dual-context-files.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Model-Agnostic Repo Setup: Dual Context Files (CLAUDE.md + AGENTS.md)\"\naliases: [AGENTS.md dual file, CLAUDE.md dual file, model-agnostic repo, Codex handoff checklist, pre-commit sync hook, hooksPath, import approach docs, PLATFORMS.md, CLAUDE.md import pointer, mirror approach docs, @AGENTS.md stub, root CLAUDE.md consolidation, hook-enforced mirror, untracked files worktree caveat, AGENTS.md routing table, thin adapter model files, multi-model routing table, GEMINI.md adapter, one-hop routing table, three-model adapter]\ntags: [claude-code, tooling, git, codex, openai, workflow, configuration, multi-model]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-05-22.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-05-26.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-05-28.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-02.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-05-22\nupdated: 2026-06-16\n---\n\n# Model-Agnostic Repo Setup: Dual Context Files (CLAUDE.md + AGENTS.md)\n\nOn 2026-05-22, the FaceComps repo was configured to work with both Claude Code and OpenAI Codex by maintaining two standalone context files — `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` — backed by a tracked pre-commit hook that blocks commits when the files diverge (the \"mirror approach\"). On 2026-05-26, a different approach was recommended: treating `AGENTS.md` as the single source of truth with `CLAUDE.md` as an `@import` pointer (the \"import approach\"), explicitly allowing the files to diverge with tool-specific content, and retiring the mirror divergence hook in favor of a planned `PLATFORMS.md` switching guide. On 2026-05-28, the import approach was applied concretely to the Baylor Load Simulator root: the root `CLAUDE.md`, which had lagged as a parallel copy of `AGENTS.md`, was collapsed to a one-line `@AGENTS.md` stub — matching the pattern already in use under the `simulator/` subdirectory. On 2026-06-02, working in the FaceComps repo confirmed a critical constraint: when a repo's pre-commit hook **enforces** byte-identity via staged blob hash comparison, the import pattern is structurally incompatible — the hook contract is non-negotiable and overrides the preference framework. On 2026-06-11, the KeystoneOS project established the mature form of the import approach for multi-model repositories: `AGENTS.md` as a canonical **one-hop routing table** with `CLAUDE.md` and `GEMINI.md` as thin adapters that defer to it for all shared project context.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Mirror approach (2026-05-22):** `AGENTS.md` is a full standalone copy of `CLAUDE.md`; a tracked `.githooks/pre-commit` hook blocks commits when the two files have diverged (detected via staged blob hash comparison). Activate on each fresh clone with `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`; bypass intentionally with `--no-verify`. Commits shipped to `origin/main` on 2026-05-22: `AGENTS.md` (`d9ee159`), `.gitignore` update (`46ffec9`), pre-commit hook (`2e57390`).\n- **Standalone copies chosen over includes/symlinks (2026-05-22):** `@include` pointers and symlinks were explicitly rejected at setup time because include syntax is not standardized across tools (a Claude Code `@import` may be invisible to Codex) and symlinks require filesystem support that may not exist in all environments; full standalone duplication keeps the two files identical now while allowing future tool-specific divergence.\n- **Import approach (2026-05-26):** `AGENTS.md` is the single source of truth for shared project context; `CLAUDE.md` contains an `@import` pointer to `AGENTS.md` plus Claude Code-specific annotations (hook event names, skill syntax, MCP references). A `PLATFORMS.md` switching guide explains which file each tool reads and how to hand off between Claude Code and Codex. The pre-commit mirror hook is retired because the two files are expected to legitimately differ.\n- **Root stub consolidation (2026-05-28):** In the Baylor Load Simulator repo, `simulator/CLAUDE.md` already used the import approach (pointing to `simulator/AGENTS.md`), but the repo root `CLAUDE.md` had silently diverged into a full parallel copy of root `AGENTS.md`. This was collapsed to a single one-line `@AGENTS.md` stub. The lesson: in monorepos with subdirectories, every `CLAUDE.md` instance — root and subdirectory alike — must consistently use the chosen approach; a lagging parallel copy creates the same drift hazard as the mirror approach without the protection of the pre-commit divergence hook.\n- **Hook-enforced mirror (2026-06-02, FaceComps):** When a pre-commit hook enforces byte-identical `CLAUDE.md` = `AGENTS.md` via staged blob hash comparison, the import pattern is structurally incompatible — a one-line `@AGENTS.md` stub will always fail the hash check against a full `AGENTS.md`. In such repos, the mirror pattern is the only viable option and the hook contract takes precedence over the preference framework. The FaceComps repo reconciled both files to byte-identical mirrors (`commit 8815925`) for exactly this reason.\n- **One-hop routing table for multi-model repos (2026-06-11):** In a repository targeting multiple AI model ecosystems (e.g., both Claude Code's `CLAUDE.md` and Gemini's `GEMINI.md`), `AGENTS.md` serves as a canonical one-hop routing table — all model-specific project questions resolve by reading `AGENTS.md` plus the appropriate thin adapter file. `CLAUDE.md` and `GEMINI.md` become minimal wrapper files containing only tool-specific syntax and invocation conventions; all shared project context is centralized in `AGENTS.md`. This is the import approach taken to its logical conclusion for multi-model repositories.\n- **Pre-commit hook behavior detail:** the `.githooks/pre-commit` hook compares *staged blob hashes* of `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md`, not working-tree bytes. When editing either file, both must be staged simultaneously before committing, or the hook will block the commit on a false-positive divergence.\n- **Untracked-files caveat for worktree isolation:** when a repo contains important untracked source-data directories (e.g., FaceComps's `SourceFaces/SCRIPTS/`), working directly on `main` may be preferable to a worktree because those untracked files would be absent in a fresh worktree. Worktree isolation should be skipped when the work requires access to untracked files that cannot be brought into the worktree.\n- **When to use which approach:** use the mirror approach when the two files will be semantically identical (same instructions, different filenames, full compatibility required), or when a pre-commit hook enforces byte-identity; use the import approach when the files are expected to legitimately diverge with tool-specific content and no enforcement hook exists; use the one-hop routing table when the repo targets three or more model ecosystems and centralized routing clarity is the primary goal.\n- **Pre-handoff checklist for Codex:** before switching from Claude Code to OpenAI Codex on the same task, verify: branch is clean, `origin` is in sync, `AGENTS.md` is present and current, any required hook is configured, `.env.local` exists, and the Codex CLI is installed.\n\n## Details\n\nThe mirror approach (2026-05-22) reflects the starting assumption that Claude Code and Codex need identical context — same instructions, same project documentation — with only the filename differing per tool convention. Standalone copies were chosen over `@include` or symlinks because tool-specific include syntax is not standardized: a Claude Code `@import` pointer that Claude Code resolves correctly may be invisible to Codex or a future agent runtime, while a symlink requires filesystem support that may not exist in all environments. The pre-commit hook provides a continuous integration check at the commit boundary, making divergence a locally-caught error rather than silent drift. Because the hook lives in a tracked `.githooks/` directory, it is part of the repo's history and available to all contributors. The `git config core.hooksPath .githooks` activation step is a one-time setup on each new clone; both `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` should document this requirement. The 2026-05-22 session also added `.claude/worktrees/` to `.gitignore` to keep ephemeral worktree artifacts from being tracked, and closed with a Codex pre-handoff checklist plus an open item to add a `TODO.md` for follow-ups that live outside any file Codex reads.\n\nThe import approach (2026-05-26) acknowledges that `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` are expected to diverge as tooling matures: Claude Code-specific annotations (hook event names, skill invocation syntax, MCP tool references) are meaningless to Codex, and Codex-specific task framing (step-by-step output constraints, sandbox restrictions) may not belong in the Claude Code context file. Rather than a mirror that enforces identity, the import approach designates `AGENTS.md` as the canonical document and makes `CLAUDE.md` a pointer-plus-extensions file. This removes the need for a divergence-checking hook (the files are allowed to differ by design) and moves cross-platform guidance into an explicit `PLATFORMS.md` document.\n\nThe 2026-05-28 application of the import approach to the Baylor Load Simulator root reveals a subtle real-world drift risk: the `simulator/` subdirectory had already adopted the import pattern (its own `CLAUDE.md` was a one-line stub pointing to `simulator/AGENTS.md`), but the repo root's `CLAUDE.md` had not been updated to match and had grown into a full duplicate of root `AGENTS.md`. Because the mirror approach's pre-commit hook was not active in this repo, there was no automated check to catch the divergence — any hand-edit to one file would silently break the cross-tool handoff without warning. Collapsing the root `CLAUDE.md` to a `@AGENTS.md` stub eliminates this hidden risk and makes the repo's context-file pattern consistent at every level. The practical rule: when adopting the import approach, perform an audit of every `CLAUDE.md` in the repo (root and subdirectory) to ensure all instances use the stub form before assuming the pattern is fully applied.\n\nThe 2026-06-02 FaceComps session produced a fourth case that closes an implicit gap in the mirror/import preference framework: what happens when a pre-commit hook *enforces* byte-identity rather than merely recommending it? In FaceComps, the `.githooks/pre-commit` hook compares staged blob hashes of `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` and blocks commits when they diverge. A one-line `@AGENTS.md` import stub produces a blob hash that is categorically different from the full `AGENTS.md` blob — the hook would reject every commit on the branch. Reconciling both files to byte-identical mirrors (`commit 8815925`) was the only way to satisfy the hook contract. This case also surfaced a related worktree consideration: FaceComps has untracked source-data files in `SourceFaces/SCRIPTS/` that are essential to the active work but would be absent in a fresh worktree. Working directly on `main` (authorized by the user) was the pragmatic response; worktree isolation is appropriate when the branch can stand alone, but not when the work depends on untracked files that live only in the main working tree.\n\nThe 2026-06-11 KeystoneOS session established the most advanced form of the import approach: the one-hop routing table. In a repository serving three or more model ecosystems, the import approach's \"AGENTS.md as source of truth\" framing gains a more operational name — a routing table — because the primary design goal is not merely correctness but navigational efficiency. Any model opening the repository for the first time should be able to resolve every context question (project structure, active files, conventions, active projects) in exactly two reads: AGENTS.md (the routing table) and its own adapter file (CLAUDE.md or GEMINI.md). The \"10 cold-agent routing questions\" validation performed on 2026-06-11 operationalized this standard: ten representative questions were posed from a cold-agent starting state, and each resolved correctly in two file reads. CLAUDE.md and GEMINI.md were reduced to thin adapters containing only the tool-specific invocation syntax (hook event names for Claude Code; Gemini-specific model configuration for Gemini) while all project context, active project list, and routing logic moved to AGENTS.md. This also means that improving project routing — adding a new project, updating a project description, adding a cross-project relationship — requires editing only AGENTS.md rather than keeping multiple adapter files in sync.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas]] — The `.claude/worktrees/` gitignore addition was motivated in part by the `ExitWorktree remove` orphan-commit behavior documented there; both gotchas surfaced in the same 2026-05-22 session.\n- [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-fall-camp-mode]] — The 2026-05-26 simulator session where the import approach was recommended, and where the pre-commit hook's staged-blob-hash behavior was clarified while completing documentation tasks; the 2026-05-28 root stub consolidation completes what that session started.\n- [[concepts/project-docs-file-map-pattern]] — The 2026-05-28 documentation session that consolidated the root CLAUDE.md stub also added file-map sections to the simulator's AGENTS.md, illustrating both patterns being applied in the same pass.\n- [[concepts/fall-camp-rep-extraction-pipeline]] — The FaceComps repo work that surfaced the hook-enforced mirror constraint and untracked-files worktree caveat on 2026-06-02.\n- [[concepts/keystoneos-project-registry]] — The KeystoneOS Round 2 session (2026-06-11) whose Phase C router rewrite established AGENTS.md as a canonical one-hop routing table with CLAUDE.md and GEMINI.md as thin adapters.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-05-22.md]] — FaceComps model-agnostic repo setup session (09:55): dual-file mirror rationale, standalone-copies-over-includes decision, pre-commit hook design, `.gitignore` worktree-artifacts update, Codex pre-handoff checklist, and the open `TODO.md` follow-up item.\n- [[daily/2026-05-26.md]] — Baylor Load Simulator cross-platform docs discussion (12:28) and Plan 3 documentation tasks (17:15): import approach recommendation, hook retirement rationale, PLATFORMS.md plan, and staged-blob-hash hook behavior detail.\n- [[daily/2026-05-28.md]] — Root CLAUDE.md stub consolidation session (14:27): parallel-copy drift hazard discovered, root `CLAUDE.md` collapsed to one-line `@AGENTS.md` import stub matching the `simulator/` pattern.\n- [[daily/2026-06-02.md]] — FaceComps practice-planning session (09:29): pre-commit hook enforcement makes import pattern incompatible, mirror reconciled to byte-identical (commit `8815925`), untracked-files worktree caveat surfaced.\n- [[daily/2026-06-11.md]] — KeystoneOS Round 2 Phase C (16:53): AGENTS.md designated canonical one-hop routing table, CLAUDE.md and GEMINI.md converted to thin adapters, all 10 cold-agent routing questions verified to resolve in one hop across the rewritten router files.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/morning-email-briefing-design.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Morning Email Briefing System Design\"\naliases: [morning briefing, email briefing, morning-briefing design spec, NEEDS REPLY, FOLLOW UPS, graceful degradation briefing]\ntags: [automation, email, productivity, workflow, morning-briefing, gmail, design-spec]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-05-21.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-08.md\"\ncreated: 2026-05-21\nupdated: 2026-06-16\n---\n\n# Morning Email Briefing System Design\n\nOn 2026-05-21, a design spec for an automated morning email briefing system was written and committed to `Desktop/DEV/morning-briefing/docs/2026-05-21-morning-briefing-design.md`. The system ingests email and calendar sources, distills them into a structured 6-section briefing, and delivers the result from Gmail to the user's work Outlook inbox at approximately 07:30 each morning. A durable markdown copy is saved to the SecondBrain vault regardless of delivery status. The implementation plan was started but not completed — the session hit its context-window limit mid-generation. On 2026-06-08, a full manual inbox audit validated the 6-section taxonomy against real Outlook inbox content, confirming the category distribution and the practical utility of separating FYI/newsletters from actionable sections.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Delivery config:** trigger at 07:20 so the briefing arrives ~07:30; send **from** `eric.rash11@gmail.com` **to** the user's work Outlook address (primary inbox). First-arrival junk-filter risk for Exchange tenants: `eric.rash11@gmail.com` should be added to safe-senders to prevent the first delivery being routed to junk by Exchange's external-sender heuristics.\n- **6-section briefing format (all approved):** NEEDS REPLY, FOLLOW UPS, MEETINGS TO SCHEDULE, FORMS/TASKS OWED, STILL OPEN FROM PRIOR DAYS (age-tagged), FYI.\n- **Live validation (2026-06-08):** a full manual Outlook inbox audit produced 50 categorized items: 11 needs_reply, 3 follow_ups, 3 meetings_to_schedule, 3 forms_owed, 6 still_open, 24 FYI/newsletters. The distribution confirms the design hypothesis — FYI/newsletters dominate inbox volume (48%) but require no action, while actionable obligations are concentrated in a smaller set of items. See [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]].\n- **No auto-learning:** the allowlist and mute list are manually curated only (\"30 seconds a week\"); automated pattern-learning was deliberately rejected to keep behavior predictable and auditable.\n- **Graceful degradation:** the system must never silently skip a briefing — if a source fails, the briefing still sends with a banner explaining what could not be retrieved. Silent failures that leave the inbox empty are worse than a degraded briefing.\n- **Durable markdown copy:** each day's briefing is saved to `Desktop/SecondBrain/morning-briefings/YYYY-MM-DD.md` regardless of email delivery outcome — the vault copy is a non-negotiable design requirement, not an optional log.\n- **Self-review caught a spec gap:** a checklist self-review step during implementation plan generation identified that carryover items (STILL OPEN FROM PRIOR DAYS) were not being matched against today's new email threads for resolution detection — a behavioral omission in spec Section 8 that would have caused stale items to accumulate even after they were addressed in follow-up threads. The session hit the context-window limit while patching this gap, leaving the implementation plan incomplete.\n\n## Details\n\nThe design spec's graceful-degradation principle is the most architecturally significant decision in the system. Making the briefing always send *something* — even a degraded version with a banner such as \"⚠ Calendar source unavailable today\" — is a conscious choice to prefer visible, annotated failures over silent ones. A pipeline that fails silently produces no signal that something broke; a degraded briefing with an explanatory banner tells the user what the system intended and why it could not fully deliver. This framing also means the implementation must treat each source (email, calendar, carryover state) as independently degradable — no single source failure should prevent the briefing from sending.\n\nThe self-review step during implementation plan generation caught a real behavioral defect in the spec before any code was written. The `writing-plans` skill includes a review checklist pass; that pass identified that the STILL OPEN section's carryover logic (spec Section 8) lacked a mechanism for detecting when a prior-day item had since been addressed by a new email thread — a gap that would have caused the carryover list to grow monotonically rather than shrinking as items were resolved. This is a planning-layer instance of the same principle documented in [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]]: a second review pass with a different focus (behavioral completeness rather than format compliance) catches what the initial drafting pass misses. Two items remain outstanding before implementation can proceed: the work Outlook email address (currently a placeholder in the spec), and completion of the implementation plan (left mid-write when the session expired).\n\nThe 2026-06-08 live inbox audit (see [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]]) validated the 6-section taxonomy against a real Outlook inbox. The distribution — 11 needs_reply, 3 follow_ups, 3 meetings_to_schedule, 3 forms_owed, 6 still_open, 24 FYI/newsletters — confirms that FYI/newsletters dominate inbox volume (48%) but require no action, while actionable items are concentrated across the 5 actionable categories. This empirical validation supports the design decision to separate FYI from actionable sections rather than interleaving them. The same structured 6-category taxonomy drives both the manual triage process and this automated daily briefing, making the taxonomy the stable architectural element across both tools — see [[connections/inbox-triage-and-morning-briefing-taxonomy]].\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub]] — The durable `SecondBrain/morning-briefings/YYYY-MM-DD.md` copy is a third independent example of routing structured output into the Obsidian vault as an architectural invariant, independent of the delivery channel.\n- [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] — The self-review checklist catching the carryover-resolution spec gap is a planning-layer instance of the \"second pass with a different focus catches blind spots\" pattern.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — A related morning-orientation pipeline (built 2026-06-10) that also generates a daily briefing-style summary and stores it in the SecondBrain vault; the two systems address the same goal (structured daily orientation) at complementary layers — the on-ramp distills OKRs and must-dos from a script, while this briefing distills email and calendar into action categories.\n- [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]] — The manual ad-hoc counterpart to this automated daily system; both use the same 6-category taxonomy, making triage output directly consumable as input to the morning briefing pipeline without schema conversion.\n- [[connections/inbox-triage-and-morning-briefing-taxonomy]] — The cross-cutting connection documenting how the 6-category taxonomy spans this briefing system, the manual inbox triage, and the on-ramp automation as a shared architectural contract.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-05-21.md]] — Design spec session (17:54): delivery config, 6-section format, graceful degradation, SecondBrain copy, allowlist policy, and self-review spec gap catch.\n- [[daily/2026-06-08.md]] — Live inbox audit (08:04): 6-section taxonomy validated against real Outlook inbox with 50-item distribution (11/3/3/3/6/24), confirming the design's section structure and FYI-dominance hypothesis.\n\n```\n\nThese are the 13 articles (of 314) most related to this log, plus graph neighbours and least-linked articles. This is the relevant context, not a partial view to be cautious about.\n- The Current Wiki Index above lists EVERY article. Consult it before creating a new one, and you may [[wikilink]] any article listed there even if its text is not shown.\n- If this log adds detail, correction, or a new example to an article shown above, UPDATE it: re-emit it at its existing path with the full revised content. Updating existing articles is a primary expected outcome, not a fallback.\n\n## Daily Log to Compile\n\n**File:** 2026-09-30.md\n\n# Daily Log: 2026-09-30\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Memory Flush (11:29)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Memory Flush (12:13)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (12:33)\n\n**Context:** End-of-day upkeep on the Claude automation/memory stack (Mac), then `/closeout`.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Stack doctor run showed all 5 scheduled jobs OK, empty error log, both machines in step with GitHub — a clear improvement over 9/28 (backup job failing, 5 errors).\n- Doctor-plus context audit: all 4 problems from 8/29 fixed, 1 of 12 stranded memory-save files left, project memory index had grown to ~8,200 chars.\n- User chose both offered fixes: \"archive it and trim the index\".\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Archived the stranded 9/21 session-flush file to `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/scripts/_stranded-archive/` rather than recovering it — 9 days old, and recovery would date its facts as today. Kept Mac-local (never git-tracked).\n- Trimmed the project memory index from 8,162 → 5,549 chars (~30% smaller). Each line now states only *when to open* a file; details (dates, counts, error text) stay in the memory files. All 56 links verified.\n- Committed and pushed immediately rather than waiting on the 21:00 backup, per user request.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- The 7:51 backup failed because `global-memory` was behind GitHub — a repo out of sync silently blocks the backup job.\n- The project memory index loads every session, so its size is a recurring per-session context cost. Line format \"when to open this file\" is the fix that keeps it small.\n- Closeout's full-DEV sweep caught dirty repos from other sessions: `DEV/CommandCentre` (`server.out`) and `DEV/graphify-out` (graph snapshots).\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Tomorrow: confirm the 21:00 backup ran clean — that clears the last stack-doctor warning (the `backup-error` entry). The `flush-error` entry from 9/27 (usage limit) will age off on its own.\n- `~/.claude/skills/.trash/` (3 deleted files) left uncommitted on purpose — decide later.\n\n**Commits:** `ebfb874` + `bb3ce1b` (dotclaude), `4dc349d` (claude-skills), `a2f4f78` (claude-global-memory), `3cae9d2` (CommandCentre), `d4d1b5f` (graphify-out). Report at `~/.claude/skills/doctor-plus/findings-2026-09-30.md`.\n\n\n\n## Your Task\n\nDo NOT use any tools and do NOT attempt to write files — your sandbox cannot\nwrite under this directory. Instead, emit the articles as plain text using the\nexact delimiter format below. The orchestrator parses your output and writes the\nfiles itself.\n\n### Output format\n\nFor each new or updated article, emit a block:\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: complete YAML frontmatter + body, per the AGENTS.md schema>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nUse `connections/<slug>.md` for connection articles and `qa/<slug>.md` for Q&A.\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated. To UPDATE an existing article, emit it with\nits existing path and the full revised content (it overwrites).\n\nAfter the article blocks, emit exactly one index block and one log block:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/<slug>]] | one-line summary | 2026-09-30.md | 2026-10-01 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-10-01T06:08:52-05:00] compile | 2026-09-30.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-30.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/<slug>]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n===END LOG===\n\n### Rules\n1. Identify 3-7 distinct concepts worth their own article.\n2. Emit ===NOTHING=== ONLY when the log itself contains no durable knowledge\n   (e.g. \"FLUSH_OK\", or purely transient chatter). \"An article already covers\n   this topic\" is NOT a reason to emit ===NOTHING=== - it is a reason to UPDATE\n   that article with whatever this log adds.\n3. Every article: complete YAML frontmatter (title, sources, created, updated)\n   with `sources` citing 2026-09-30.md; at least 2 [[wikilinks]]; Key Points\n   3-5 bullets; Details 2+ paragraphs; Related Concepts 2+ entries; encyclopedia\n   style — neutral and comprehensive. Follow the AGENTS.md article format exactly.\n4. Create a connection article when the log reveals a non-obvious relationship\n   between 2+ concepts.\n4b. Do NOT re-emit an article whose only change would be the `updated:` date or\n   a restatement of what it already says. Measured on a real run, 4 of 5\n   \"updates\" changed nothing but the timestamp - that is churn: it rewrites\n   files, dirties backups, and invalidates caches while adding no knowledge.\n   Re-emit an article only when you are adding substantive content.\n5. Add one ===INDEX=== table row per article (new or updated).\n\nEmit ONLY the delimiter blocks — no preamble, no commentary.\n"},"uuid":"18062a3b-9733-4e72-a47a-4fcbd5d8f743","timestamp":"2026-10-01T11:08:53.606Z","permissionMode":"default","promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"972285da-8a0d-4458-8c9b-e31335054cdf","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"18062a3b-9733-4e72-a47a-4fcbd5d8f743","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/AGENTS.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/AGENTS.md","content":"# AGENTS.md - Personal Knowledge Base Schema\n\n> Adapted from [Andrej Karpathy's LLM Knowledge Base](https://gist.github.com/karpathy/442a6bf555914893e9891c11519de94f) architecture.\n> Instead of ingesting external articles, this system compiles knowledge from your own AI conversations.\n\n## The Compiler Analogy\n\n```\ndaily/          = source code    (your conversations - the raw material)\nLLM             = compiler       (extracts and organizes knowledge)\nknowledge/      = executable     (structured, queryable knowledge base)\nlint            = test suite     (health checks for consistency)\nmemlint         = test suite     (same, for the memory files loaded each session)\nqueries         = runtime        (using the knowledge)\n```\n\nYou don't manually organize your knowledge. You have conversations, and the LLM handles the synthesis, cross-referencing, and maintenance.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture\n\n### Layer 1: `daily/` - Conversation Logs (Immutable Source)\n\nDaily logs capture what happened in your AI coding sessions. These are the \"raw sources\" - append-only, never edited after the fact.\n\n```\ndaily/\n├── 2026-04-01.md\n├── 2026-04-02.md\n├── ...\n```\n\nEach file follows this format:\n\n```markdown\n# Daily Log: YYYY-MM-DD\n\n## Sessions\n\n### Session (HH:MM) - Brief Title\n\n**Context:** What the user was working on.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- User asked about X, assistant explained Y\n- Decided to use Z approach because...\n- Discovered that W doesn't work when...\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Chose library X over Y because...\n- Architecture: went with pattern Z\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Always do X before Y to avoid...\n- The gotcha with Z is that...\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [ ] Follow up on X\n- [ ] Refactor Y when time permits\n```\n\n### Layer 2: `knowledge/` - Compiled Knowledge (LLM-Owned)\n\nThe LLM owns this directory entirely. Humans read it but rarely edit it directly.\n\n```\nknowledge/\n├── index.md              # Master catalog - every article with one-line summary\n├── log.md                # Append-only chronological build log\n├── concepts/             # Atomic knowledge articles\n├── connections/          # Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n└── qa/                   # Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n```\n\n### Layer 3: This File (AGENTS.md)\n\nThe schema that tells the LLM how to compile and maintain the knowledge base. This is the \"compiler specification.\"\n\n---\n\n## Structural Files\n\n### `knowledge/index.md` - Master Catalog\n\nA table listing every knowledge article. This is the primary retrieval mechanism - the LLM reads this FIRST when answering any query, then selects relevant articles to read in full.\n\nFormat:\n\n```markdown\n# Knowledge Base Index\n\n| Article | Summary | Compiled From | Updated |\n|---------|---------|---------------|---------|\n| [[concepts/supabase-auth]] | Row-level security patterns and JWT gotchas | daily/2026-04-02.md | 2026-04-02 |\n| [[connections/auth-and-webhooks]] | Token verification patterns shared across Supabase auth and Stripe webhooks | daily/2026-04-02.md, daily/2026-04-04.md | 2026-04-04 |\n```\n\n### `knowledge/log.md` - Build Log\n\nAppend-only chronological record of every compile, query, and lint operation.\n\nFormat:\n\n```markdown\n# Build Log\n\n## [2026-04-01T14:30:00] compile | Daily Log 2026-04-01\n- Source: daily/2026-04-01.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/nextjs-project-structure]], [[concepts/tailwind-setup]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n\n## [2026-04-02T09:00:00] query | \"How do I handle auth redirects?\"\n- Consulted: [[concepts/supabase-auth]], [[concepts/nextjs-middleware]]\n- Filed to: [[qa/auth-redirect-handling]]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Article Formats\n\n### Concept Articles (`knowledge/concepts/`)\n\nOne article per atomic piece of knowledge. These are facts, patterns, decisions, preferences, and lessons extracted from your conversations.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Concept Name\"\naliases: [alternate-name, abbreviation]\ntags: [domain, topic]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-01.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-03.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-01\nupdated: 2026-04-03\n---\n\n# Concept Name\n\n[2-4 sentence core explanation]\n\n## Key Points\n\n- [Bullet points, each self-contained]\n\n## Details\n\n[Deeper explanation, encyclopedia-style paragraphs]\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/related-concept]] - How it connects\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-01.md]] - Initial discovery during project setup\n- [[daily/2026-04-03.md]] - Updated after debugging session\n```\n\n### Connection Articles (`knowledge/connections/`)\n\nCross-cutting synthesis linking 2+ concepts. Created when a conversation reveals a non-obvious relationship.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: X and Y\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/concept-x\"\n  - \"concepts/concept-y\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-04.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-04\nupdated: 2026-04-04\n---\n\n# Connection: X and Y\n\n## The Connection\n\n[What links these concepts]\n\n## Key Insight\n\n[The non-obvious relationship discovered]\n\n## Evidence\n\n[Specific examples from conversations]\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/concept-x]]\n- [[concepts/concept-y]]\n```\n\n### Q&A Articles (`knowledge/qa/`)\n\nFiled answers from queries. Every complex question answered by the system can be permanently stored, making future queries smarter.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Q: Original Question\"\nquestion: \"The exact question asked\"\nconsulted:\n  - \"concepts/article-1\"\n  - \"concepts/article-2\"\nfiled: 2026-04-05\n---\n\n# Q: Original Question\n\n## Answer\n\n[The synthesized answer with [[wikilinks]] to sources]\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- [[concepts/article-1]] - Relevant because...\n- [[concepts/article-2]] - Provided context on...\n\n## Follow-Up Questions\n\n- What about edge case X?\n- How does this change if Y?\n```\n\n---\n\n## Core Operations\n\n### 1. Compile (daily/ -> knowledge/)\n\nWhen processing a daily log:\n\n1. Read the daily log file\n2. Read `knowledge/index.md` to understand current knowledge state\n3. Read existing articles that may need updating\n4. For each piece of knowledge found in the log:\n   - If an existing concept article covers this topic: UPDATE it with new information, add the daily log as a source\n   - If it's a new topic: CREATE a new `concepts/` article\n5. If the log reveals a non-obvious connection between 2+ existing concepts: CREATE a `connections/` article\n6. UPDATE `knowledge/index.md` with new/modified entries\n7. APPEND to `knowledge/log.md`\n\n**Important guidelines:**\n- A single daily log may touch 3-10 knowledge articles\n- Prefer updating existing articles over creating near-duplicates\n- Use Obsidian-style `[[wikilinks]]` with full relative paths from knowledge/\n- Write in encyclopedia style - factual, concise, self-contained\n- Every article must have YAML frontmatter\n- Every article must link back to its source daily logs\n\n### 2. Query (Ask the Knowledge Base)\n\n1. Read `knowledge/index.md` (the master catalog)\n2. Based on the question, identify 3-10 relevant articles from the index\n3. Read those articles in full\n4. Synthesize an answer with `[[wikilink]]` citations\n5. If `--file-back` is specified: create a `knowledge/qa/` article and update index.md and log.md\n\n**Why this works without RAG:** At personal knowledge base scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms cosine similarity. The LLM understands what the question is really asking and selects pages accordingly. Embeddings find similar words; the LLM finds relevant concepts.\n\n### 3. Lint (Health Checks)\n\nSeven checks, run periodically:\n\n1. **Broken links** - `[[wikilinks]]` pointing to non-existent articles\n2. **Orphan pages** - Articles with zero inbound links from other articles\n3. **Orphan sources** - Daily logs that haven't been compiled yet\n4. **Stale articles** - Source daily log changed since article was last compiled\n5. **Contradictions** - Conflicting claims across articles (requires LLM judgment)\n6. **Missing backlinks** - A links to B but B doesn't link back to A\n7. **Sparse articles** - Below 200 words, likely incomplete\n\nOutput: a markdown report with severity levels (error, warning, suggestion).\n\n---\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **Wikilinks:** Use Obsidian-style `[[path/to/article]]` without `.md` extension\n- **Writing style:** Encyclopedia-style, factual, third-person where appropriate\n- **Dates:** ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD for dates, full ISO for timestamps in log.md)\n- **File naming:** lowercase, hyphens for spaces (e.g., `supabase-row-level-security.md`)\n- **Frontmatter:** Every article must have YAML frontmatter with at minimum: title, sources, created, updated\n- **Sources:** Always link back to the daily log(s) that contributed to an article\n\n---\n\n## Full Project Structure\n\n```\nllm-personal-kb/\n|-- .claude/\n|   |-- settings.json                # Hook configuration (auto-activates in Claude Code)\n|-- .gitignore                       # Excludes runtime state, temp files, caches\n|-- AGENTS.md                        # This file - schema + full technical reference\n|-- README.md                        # Concise overview + quick start\n|-- pyproject.toml                   # Dependencies (at root so hooks can find it)\n|-- daily/                           # \"Source code\" - conversation logs (immutable)\n|-- knowledge/                       # \"Executable\" - compiled knowledge (LLM-owned)\n|   |-- index.md                     #   Master catalog - THE retrieval mechanism\n|   |-- log.md                       #   Append-only build log\n|   |-- concepts/                    #   Atomic knowledge articles\n|   |-- connections/                 #   Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n|   |-- qa/                          #   Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n|-- scripts/                         # CLI tools\n|   |-- compile.py                   #   Compile daily logs -> knowledge articles\n|   |-- query.py                     #   Ask questions (index-guided, no RAG)\n|   |-- lint.py                      #   7 health checks (knowledge base)\n|   |-- memlint.py                   #   6 health checks (memory tier)\n|   |-- flush.py                     #   Extract memories from conversations (background)\n|   |-- config.py                    #   Path constants\n|   |-- utils.py                     #   Shared helpers\n|-- hooks/                           # Claude Code hooks\n|   |-- session-start.py             #   Injects knowledge into every session\n|   |-- session-end.py               #   Extracts conversation -> daily log\n|   |-- pre-compact.py               #   Safety net: captures context before compaction\n|-- reports/                         # Lint reports (gitignored)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Hook System (Automatic Capture)\n\nHooks are configured in `.claude/settings.json` and fire automatically when you use Claude Code in this project.\n\n### `.claude/settings.json` Format\n\n```json\n{\n  \"hooks\": {\n    \"SessionStart\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-start.py\", \"timeout\": 15 }] }],\n    \"PreCompact\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/pre-compact.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }],\n    \"SessionEnd\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-end.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCommands use simple relative paths from the project root. Empty `matcher` catches all events.\n\n### Hook Details\n\n**`session-start.py`** (SessionStart)\n- Pure local I/O, no API calls, runs in under 1 second\n- Reads `knowledge/index.md` and the most recent daily log\n- Outputs JSON to stdout: `{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"...\"}}`\n- Claude sees the knowledge base index at the start of every session\n- Max context: 20,000 characters\n\n**`session-end.py`** (SessionEnd)\n- Reads hook input from stdin (JSON with `session_id`, `transcript_path`, `cwd`)\n- Copies the raw JSONL transcript to a temp file (no parsing in the hook - keeps it fast)\n- Spawns `flush.py` as a fully detached background process\n- Recursion guard: exits immediately if `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY` env var is set\n\n**`pre-compact.py`** (PreCompact)\n- Same architecture as session-end.py\n- Fires before Claude Code auto-compacts the context window\n- Guards against empty `transcript_path` (known Claude Code bug #13668)\n- Critical for long sessions: captures context before summarization discards it\n\n**Why both PreCompact and SessionEnd?** Long-running sessions may trigger multiple auto-compactions before you close the session. Without PreCompact, intermediate context is lost to summarization before SessionEnd ever fires.\n\n### Background Flush Process (`flush.py`)\n\nSpawned by both hooks as a fully detached background process:\n- **Windows:** `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP | DETACHED_PROCESS` flags\n- **Mac/Linux:** `start_new_session=True`\n\nThis ensures flush.py survives after Claude Code's hook process exits.\n\n**What flush.py does:**\n1. Sets `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY=memory_flush` env var (prevents recursive hook firing)\n2. Reads the pre-extracted conversation context from the temp `.md` file\n3. Skips if context is empty or if same session was flushed within 60 seconds (deduplication)\n4. Calls Claude Agent SDK (`query()` with `allowed_tools=[]`, `max_turns=2`)\n5. Claude decides what's worth saving - returns structured bullet points or `FLUSH_OK`\n6. Appends result to `daily/YYYY-MM-DD.md`\n7. Cleans up temp context file\n8. **End-of-day auto-compilation:** If it's past 6 PM local time (`COMPILE_AFTER_HOUR = 18`) and today's daily log has changed since its last compilation (hash comparison against `state.json`), spawns `compile.py` as another detached background process. This means compilation happens automatically once a day without needing a cron job or manual trigger.\n\n### JSONL Transcript Format\n\nClaude Code stores conversations as `.jsonl` files. Messages are nested under a `message` key:\n\n```python\nentry = json.loads(line)\nmsg = entry.get(\"message\", {})\nrole = msg.get(\"role\", \"\")     # \"user\" or \"assistant\"\ncontent = msg.get(\"content\", \"\")  # string or list of content blocks\n```\n\nContent can be a string or a list of blocks (`{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"...\"}` dicts).\n\n---\n\n## Script Details\n\n### compile.py - The Compiler\n\nUses the Claude Agent SDK's async streaming `query()`:\n\n```python\nasync for message in query(\n    prompt=compile_prompt,\n    options=ClaudeAgentOptions(\n        cwd=str(ROOT_DIR),\n        system_prompt={\"type\": \"preset\", \"preset\": \"claude_code\"},\n        allowed_tools=[\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Edit\", \"Glob\", \"Grep\"],\n        permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\",\n        max_turns=30,\n    ),\n):\n```\n\n- Builds a prompt with: AGENTS.md schema, current index, all existing articles, and the daily log\n- Claude reads the daily log, decides what concepts to extract, and writes files directly\n- `permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\"` auto-approves all file operations\n- Incremental: tracks SHA-256 hashes of daily logs in `state.json`, skips unchanged files\n- Cost: ~$0.45-0.65 per daily log (increases as KB grows)\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/compile.py              # compile new/changed only\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --all        # force recompile everything\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --file daily/2026-04-01.md\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --dry-run\n```\n\n### query.py - Index-Guided Retrieval\n\nLoads the entire knowledge base into context (index + all articles). No RAG.\n\nAt personal KB scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms vector similarity. The LLM understands what you're really asking; cosine similarity just finds similar words.\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/query.py \"What auth patterns do I use?\"\nuv run python scripts/query.py \"What's my error handling strategy?\" --file-back\n```\n\nWith `--file-back`, creates a Q&A article in `knowledge/qa/` and updates the index and log. This is the compounding loop - every question makes the KB smarter.\n\n### lint.py - Health Checks\n\nSeven checks:\n\n| Check | Type | Catches |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Broken links | Structural | `[[wikilinks]]` to non-existent articles |\n| Orphan pages | Structural | Articles with zero inbound links |\n| Orphan sources | Structural | Daily logs not yet compiled |\n| Stale articles | Structural | Source logs changed since compilation |\n| Missing backlinks | Structural | A links to B but B doesn't link back |\n| Sparse articles | Structural | Under 200 words |\n| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting claims across articles |\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/lint.py                    # all checks\nuv run python scripts/lint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\n```\n\nReports saved to `reports/lint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`.\n\n### memlint.py - Memory Tier Health Checks\n\n`lint.py` stops at `knowledge/`. The files the harness actually injects into every\nsession live elsewhere and were never audited: `~/.claude/global-memory/` and the\nactive `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/` slugs. Drift there is the most expensive\ndrift in the system, because it is read at the start of every conversation.\n\nSeven checks:\n\n| Check | Type | Catches |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Index drift | Structural | Memory file missing from MEMORY.md, or an index pointer with no file |\n| Broken links | Structural | `[[links]]` that do not match any frontmatter `name:` in the slug |\n| Name form | Structural | `name:` that is not the bare kebab stem (type belongs in `metadata.type`) |\n| Near-duplicates | Structural | Within-slug TF-IDF similarity above the threshold |\n| Cap overruns | Structural | A file past the character cap it declares in its own header |\n| Content in index | Structural | Facts written into MEMORY.md, which is meant to hold pointers |\n| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting, redundant, or superseded claims across memory files |\n\n**Scope.** Retired `C--Users-eric-rash*` slugs are excluded everywhere - the macOS\nslugs were re-keyed from them, so including them reports history as duplication.\n`handoffs/` and `transcripts/` are session records, not memory, and are never linted.\nThe LLM check defaults to `global-memory` + the `DEV` and `~` slugs, the ones that\nload together in a working session; `--all-slugs` widens it.\n\n**Similarity threshold.** Default 0.45. Measured 2026-09-01: the highest genuine\nwithin-slug pair scored 0.33. The report always prints the top pairs per slug so\nthe number can be retuned against evidence.\n\n**Report only.** memlint never edits a memory file. Merging and deleting memories\nstays a human decision.\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py                    # all checks\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --all-slugs        # widen the LLM check\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --threshold 0.6    # retune near-duplicates\n```\n\nReports saved to `reports/memlint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`; `--structural-only` writes\n`memlint-YYYY-MM-DD-structural.md` instead, so a free run never clobbers the\nday's paid-for LLM findings. Runs automatically after the Sunday 02:30\nadjudication job.\n\n---\n\n## State Tracking\n\n`scripts/state.json` tracks:\n- `ingested` - map of daily log filenames to SHA-256 hashes, compilation timestamps, and costs\n- `query_count` - total queries run\n- `last_lint` - timestamp of most recent lint\n- `last_memlint` - timestamp of most recent memory-tier lint\n- `total_cost` - cumulative API cost\n\n`scripts/last-flush.json` tracks flush deduplication (session_id + timestamp).\n\nBoth are gitignored and regenerated automatically.\n\n---\n\n## Dependencies\n\n`pyproject.toml` (at project root):\n- `claude-agent-sdk>=0.1.29` - Claude Agent SDK for LLM calls with tool use\n- `python-dotenv>=1.0.0` - Environment variable management\n- `tzdata>=2024.1` - Timezone data\n- Python 3.12+, managed by [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)\n\nNo API key needed - uses Claude Code's built-in credentials at `~/.claude/.credentials.json`.\n\n---\n\n## Costs\n\n| Operation | Cost |\n|-----------|------|\n| Compile one daily log | $0.45-0.65 |\n| Query (no file-back) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Query (with file-back) | ~$0.25-0.40 |\n| Full lint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Structural lint only | $0.00 |\n| Full memlint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Structural memlint only | $0.00 |\n| Memory flush (per session) | ~$0.02-0.05 |\n\n---\n\n## Customization\n\n### Additional Article Types\n\nAdd directories like `people/`, `projects/`, `tools/` to `knowledge/`. Define the article format in this file (AGENTS.md) and update `utils.py`'s `list_wiki_articles()` to include them.\n\n### Obsidian Integration\n\nThe knowledge base is pure markdown with `[[wikilinks]]` - works natively in Obsidian. Point a vault at `knowledge/` for graph view, backlinks, and search.\n\n### Scaling Beyond Index-Guided Retrieval\n\nAt ~2,000+ articles / ~2M+ tokens, the index becomes too large for the context window. At that point, add hybrid RAG (keyword + semantic search) as a retrieval layer before the LLM. See Karpathy's recommendation of `qmd` by Tobi Lutke for search at scale.\n","numLines":569,"startLine":1,"totalLines":569}},"displayPath":"AGENTS.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"988e3408-6762-4255-84cb-97cc4099db78","timestamp":"2026-10-01T11:08:53.604Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/AGENTS.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# AGENTS.md - Personal Knowledge Base Schema\n2\t\n3\t> Adapted from [Andrej Karpathy's LLM Knowledge Base](https://gist.github.com/karpathy/442a6bf555914893e9891c11519de94f) architecture.\n4\t> Instead of ingesting external articles, this system compiles knowledge from your own AI conversations.\n5\t\n6\t## The Compiler Analogy\n7\t\n8\t```\n9\tdaily/          = source code    (your conversations - the raw material)\n10\tLLM             = compiler       (extracts and organizes knowledge)\n11\tknowledge/      = executable     (structured, queryable knowledge base)\n12\tlint            = test suite     (health checks for consistency)\n13\tmemlint         = test suite     (same, for the memory files loaded each session)\n14\tqueries         = runtime        (using the knowledge)\n15\t```\n16\t\n17\tYou don't manually organize your knowledge. You have conversations, and the LLM handles the synthesis, cross-referencing, and maintenance.\n18\t\n19\t---\n20\t\n21\t## Architecture\n22\t\n23\t### Layer 1: `daily/` - Conversation Logs (Immutable Source)\n24\t\n25\tDaily logs capture what happened in your AI coding sessions. These are the \"raw sources\" - append-only, never edited after the fact.\n26\t\n27\t```\n28\tdaily/\n29\t├── 2026-04-01.md\n30\t├── 2026-04-02.md\n31\t├── ...\n32\t```\n33\t\n34\tEach file follows this format:\n35\t\n36\t```markdown\n37\t# Daily Log: YYYY-MM-DD\n38\t\n39\t## Sessions\n40\t\n41\t### Session (HH:MM) - Brief Title\n42\t\n43\t**Context:** What the user was working on.\n44\t\n45\t**Key Exchanges:**\n46\t- User asked about X, assistant explained Y\n47\t- Decided to use Z approach because...\n48\t- Discovered that W doesn't work when...\n49\t\n50\t**Decisions Made:**\n51\t- Chose library X over Y because...\n52\t- Architecture: went with pattern Z\n53\t\n54\t**Lessons Learned:**\n55\t- Always do X before Y to avoid...\n56\t- The gotcha with Z is that...\n57\t\n58\t**Action Items:**\n59\t- [ ] Follow up on X\n60\t- [ ] Refactor Y when time permits\n61\t```\n62\t\n63\t### Layer 2: `knowledge/` - Compiled Knowledge (LLM-Owned)\n64\t\n65\tThe LLM owns this directory entirely. Humans read it but rarely edit it directly.\n66\t\n67\t```\n68\tknowledge/\n69\t├── index.md              # Master catalog - every article with one-line summary\n70\t├── log.md                # Append-only chronological build log\n71\t├── concepts/             # Atomic knowledge articles\n72\t├── connections/          # Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n73\t└── qa/                   # Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n74\t```\n75\t\n76\t### Layer 3: This File (AGENTS.md)\n77\t\n78\tThe schema that tells the LLM how to compile and maintain the knowledge base. This is the \"compiler specification.\"\n79\t\n80\t---\n81\t\n82\t## Structural Files\n83\t\n84\t### `knowledge/index.md` - Master Catalog\n85\t\n86\tA table listing every knowledge article. This is the primary retrieval mechanism - the LLM reads this FIRST when answering any query, then selects relevant articles to read in full.\n87\t\n88\tFormat:\n89\t\n90\t```markdown\n91\t# Knowledge Base Index\n92\t\n93\t| Article | Summary | Compiled From | Updated |\n94\t|---------|---------|---------------|---------|\n95\t| [[concepts/supabase-auth]] | Row-level security patterns and JWT gotchas | daily/2026-04-02.md | 2026-04-02 |\n96\t| [[connections/auth-and-webhooks]] | Token verification patterns shared across Supabase auth and Stripe webhooks | daily/2026-04-02.md, daily/2026-04-04.md | 2026-04-04 |\n97\t```\n98\t\n99\t### `knowledge/log.md` - Build Log\n100\t\n101\tAppend-only chronological record of every compile, query, and lint operation.\n102\t\n103\tFormat:\n104\t\n105\t```markdown\n106\t# Build Log\n107\t\n108\t## [2026-04-01T14:30:00] compile | Daily Log 2026-04-01\n109\t- Source: daily/2026-04-01.md\n110\t- Articles created: [[concepts/nextjs-project-structure]], [[concepts/tailwind-setup]]\n111\t- Articles updated: (none)\n112\t\n113\t## [2026-04-02T09:00:00] query | \"How do I handle auth redirects?\"\n114\t- Consulted: [[concepts/supabase-auth]], [[concepts/nextjs-middleware]]\n115\t- Filed to: [[qa/auth-redirect-handling]]\n116\t```\n117\t\n118\t---\n119\t\n120\t## Article Formats\n121\t\n122\t### Concept Articles (`knowledge/concepts/`)\n123\t\n124\tOne article per atomic piece of knowledge. These are facts, patterns, decisions, preferences, and lessons extracted from your conversations.\n125\t\n126\t```markdown\n127\t---\n128\ttitle: \"Concept Name\"\n129\taliases: [alternate-name, abbreviation]\n130\ttags: [domain, topic]\n131\tsources:\n132\t  - \"daily/2026-04-01.md\"\n133\t  - \"daily/2026-04-03.md\"\n134\tcreated: 2026-04-01\n135\tupdated: 2026-04-03\n136\t---\n137\t\n138\t# Concept Name\n139\t\n140\t[2-4 sentence core explanation]\n141\t\n142\t## Key Points\n143\t\n144\t- [Bullet points, each self-contained]\n145\t\n146\t## Details\n147\t\n148\t[Deeper explanation, encyclopedia-style paragraphs]\n149\t\n150\t## Related Concepts\n151\t\n152\t- [[concepts/related-concept]] - How it connects\n153\t\n154\t## Sources\n155\t\n156\t- [[daily/2026-04-01.md]] - Initial discovery during project setup\n157\t- [[daily/2026-04-03.md]] - Updated after debugging session\n158\t```\n159\t\n160\t### Connection Articles (`knowledge/connections/`)\n161\t\n162\tCross-cutting synthesis linking 2+ concepts. Created when a conversation reveals a non-obvious relationship.\n163\t\n164\t```markdown\n165\t---\n166\ttitle: \"Connection: X and Y\"\n167\tconnects:\n168\t  - \"concepts/concept-x\"\n169\t  - \"concepts/concept-y\"\n170\tsources:\n171\t  - \"daily/2026-04-04.md\"\n172\tcreated: 2026-04-04\n173\tupdated: 2026-04-04\n174\t---\n175\t\n176\t# Connection: X and Y\n177\t\n178\t## The Connection\n179\t\n180\t[What links these concepts]\n181\t\n182\t## Key Insight\n183\t\n184\t[The non-obvious relationship discovered]\n185\t\n186\t## Evidence\n187\t\n188\t[Specific examples from conversations]\n189\t\n190\t## Related Concepts\n191\t\n192\t- [[concepts/concept-x]]\n193\t- [[concepts/concept-y]]\n194\t```\n195\t\n196\t### Q&A Articles (`knowledge/qa/`)\n197\t\n198\tFiled answers from queries. Every complex question answered by the system can be permanently stored, making future queries smarter.\n199\t\n200\t```markdown\n201\t---\n202\ttitle: \"Q: Original Question\"\n203\tquestion: \"The exact question asked\"\n204\tconsulted:\n205\t  - \"concepts/article-1\"\n206\t  - \"concepts/article-2\"\n207\tfiled: 2026-04-05\n208\t---\n209\t\n210\t# Q: Original Question\n211\t\n212\t## Answer\n213\t\n214\t[The synthesized answer with [[wikilinks]] to sources]\n215\t\n216\t## Sources Consulted\n217\t\n218\t- [[concepts/article-1]] - Relevant because...\n219\t- [[concepts/article-2]] - Provided context on...\n220\t\n221\t## Follow-Up Questions\n222\t\n223\t- What about edge case X?\n224\t- How does this change if Y?\n225\t```\n226\t\n227\t---\n228\t\n229\t## Core Operations\n230\t\n231\t### 1. Compile (daily/ -> knowledge/)\n232\t\n233\tWhen processing a daily log:\n234\t\n235\t1. Read the daily log file\n236\t2. Read `knowledge/index.md` to understand current knowledge state\n237\t3. Read existing articles that may need updating\n238\t4. For each piece of knowledge found in the log:\n239\t   - If an existing concept article covers this topic: UPDATE it with new information, add the daily log as a source\n240\t   - If it's a new topic: CREATE a new `concepts/` article\n241\t5. If the log reveals a non-obvious connection between 2+ existing concepts: CREATE a `connections/` article\n242\t6. UPDATE `knowledge/index.md` with new/modified entries\n243\t7. APPEND to `knowledge/log.md`\n244\t\n245\t**Important guidelines:**\n246\t- A single daily log may touch 3-10 knowledge articles\n247\t- Prefer updating existing articles over creating near-duplicates\n248\t- Use Obsidian-style `[[wikilinks]]` with full relative paths from knowledge/\n249\t- Write in encyclopedia style - factual, concise, self-contained\n250\t- Every article must have YAML frontmatter\n251\t- Every article must link back to its source daily logs\n252\t\n253\t### 2. Query (Ask the Knowledge Base)\n254\t\n255\t1. Read `knowledge/index.md` (the master catalog)\n256\t2. Based on the question, identify 3-10 relevant articles from the index\n257\t3. Read those articles in full\n258\t4. Synthesize an answer with `[[wikilink]]` citations\n259\t5. If `--file-back` is specified: create a `knowledge/qa/` article and update index.md and log.md\n260\t\n261\t**Why this works without RAG:** At personal knowledge base scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms cosine similarity. The LLM understands what the question is really asking and selects pages accordingly. Embeddings find similar words; the LLM finds relevant concepts.\n262\t\n263\t### 3. Lint (Health Checks)\n264\t\n265\tSeven checks, run periodically:\n266\t\n267\t1. **Broken links** - `[[wikilinks]]` pointing to non-existent articles\n268\t2. **Orphan pages** - Articles with zero inbound links from other articles\n269\t3. **Orphan sources** - Daily logs that haven't been compiled yet\n270\t4. **Stale articles** - Source daily log changed since article was last compiled\n271\t5. **Contradictions** - Conflicting claims across articles (requires LLM judgment)\n272\t6. **Missing backlinks** - A links to B but B doesn't link back to A\n273\t7. **Sparse articles** - Below 200 words, likely incomplete\n274\t\n275\tOutput: a markdown report with severity levels (error, warning, suggestion).\n276\t\n277\t---\n278\t\n279\t## Conventions\n280\t\n281\t- **Wikilinks:** Use Obsidian-style `[[path/to/article]]` without `.md` extension\n282\t- **Writing style:** Encyclopedia-style, factual, third-person where appropriate\n283\t- **Dates:** ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD for dates, full ISO for timestamps in log.md)\n284\t- **File naming:** lowercase, hyphens for spaces (e.g., `supabase-row-level-security.md`)\n285\t- **Frontmatter:** Every article must have YAML frontmatter with at minimum: title, sources, created, updated\n286\t- **Sources:** Always link back to the daily log(s) that contributed to an article\n287\t\n288\t---\n289\t\n290\t## Full Project Structure\n291\t\n292\t```\n293\tllm-personal-kb/\n294\t|-- .claude/\n295\t|   |-- settings.json                # Hook configuration (auto-activates in Claude Code)\n296\t|-- .gitignore                       # Excludes runtime state, temp files, caches\n297\t|-- AGENTS.md                        # This file - schema + full technical reference\n298\t|-- README.md                        # Concise overview + quick start\n299\t|-- pyproject.toml                   # Dependencies (at root so hooks can find it)\n300\t|-- daily/                           # \"Source code\" - conversation logs (immutable)\n301\t|-- knowledge/                       # \"Executable\" - compiled knowledge (LLM-owned)\n302\t|   |-- index.md                     #   Master catalog - THE retrieval mechanism\n303\t|   |-- log.md                       #   Append-only build log\n304\t|   |-- concepts/                    #   Atomic knowledge articles\n305\t|   |-- connections/                 #   Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n306\t|   |-- qa/                          #   Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n307\t|-- scripts/                         # CLI tools\n308\t|   |-- compile.py                   #   Compile daily logs -> knowledge articles\n309\t|   |-- query.py                     #   Ask questions (index-guided, no RAG)\n310\t|   |-- lint.py                      #   7 health checks (knowledge base)\n311\t|   |-- memlint.py                   #   6 health checks (memory tier)\n312\t|   |-- flush.py                     #   Extract memories from conversations (background)\n313\t|   |-- config.py                    #   Path constants\n314\t|   |-- utils.py                     #   Shared helpers\n315\t|-- hooks/                           # Claude Code hooks\n316\t|   |-- session-start.py             #   Injects knowledge into every session\n317\t|   |-- session-end.py               #   Extracts conversation -> daily log\n318\t|   |-- pre-compact.py               #   Safety net: captures context before compaction\n319\t|-- reports/                         # Lint reports (gitignored)\n320\t```\n321\t\n322\t---\n323\t\n324\t## Hook System (Automatic Capture)\n325\t\n326\tHooks are configured in `.claude/settings.json` and fire automatically when you use Claude Code in this project.\n327\t\n328\t### `.claude/settings.json` Format\n329\t\n330\t```json\n331\t{\n332\t  \"hooks\": {\n333\t    \"SessionStart\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-start.py\", \"timeout\": 15 }] }],\n334\t    \"PreCompact\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/pre-compact.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }],\n335\t    \"SessionEnd\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-end.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }]\n336\t  }\n337\t}\n338\t```\n339\t\n340\tCommands use simple relative paths from the project root. Empty `matcher` catches all events.\n341\t\n342\t### Hook Details\n343\t\n344\t**`session-start.py`** (SessionStart)\n345\t- Pure local I/O, no API calls, runs in under 1 second\n346\t- Reads `knowledge/index.md` and the most recent daily log\n347\t- Outputs JSON to stdout: `{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"...\"}}`\n348\t- Claude sees the knowledge base index at the start of every session\n349\t- Max context: 20,000 characters\n350\t\n351\t**`session-end.py`** (SessionEnd)\n352\t- Reads hook input from stdin (JSON with `session_id`, `transcript_path`, `cwd`)\n353\t- Copies the raw JSONL transcript to a temp file (no parsing in the hook - keeps it fast)\n354\t- Spawns `flush.py` as a fully detached background process\n355\t- Recursion guard: exits immediately if `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY` env var is set\n356\t\n357\t**`pre-compact.py`** (PreCompact)\n358\t- Same architecture as session-end.py\n359\t- Fires before Claude Code auto-compacts the context window\n360\t- Guards against empty `transcript_path` (known Claude Code bug #13668)\n361\t- Critical for long sessions: captures context before summarization discards it\n362\t\n363\t**Why both PreCompact and SessionEnd?** Long-running sessions may trigger multiple auto-compactions before you close the session. Without PreCompact, intermediate context is lost to summarization before SessionEnd ever fires.\n364\t\n365\t### Background Flush Process (`flush.py`)\n366\t\n367\tSpawned by both hooks as a fully detached background process:\n368\t- **Windows:** `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP | DETACHED_PROCESS` flags\n369\t- **Mac/Linux:** `start_new_session=True`\n370\t\n371\tThis ensures flush.py survives after Claude Code's hook process exits.\n372\t\n373\t**What flush.py does:**\n374\t1. Sets `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY=memory_flush` env var (prevents recursive hook firing)\n375\t2. Reads the pre-extracted conversation context from the temp `.md` file\n376\t3. Skips if context is empty or if same session was flushed within 60 seconds (deduplication)\n377\t4. Calls Claude Agent SDK (`query()` with `allowed_tools=[]`, `max_turns=2`)\n378\t5. Claude decides what's worth saving - returns structured bullet points or `FLUSH_OK`\n379\t6. Appends result to `daily/YYYY-MM-DD.md`\n380\t7. Cleans up temp context file\n381\t8. **End-of-day auto-compilation:** If it's past 6 PM local time (`COMPILE_AFTER_HOUR = 18`) and today's daily log has changed since its last compilation (hash comparison against `state.json`), spawns `compile.py` as another detached background process. This means compilation happens automatically once a day without needing a cron job or manual trigger.\n382\t\n383\t### JSONL Transcript Format\n384\t\n385\tClaude Code stores conversations as `.jsonl` files. Messages are nested under a `message` key:\n386\t\n387\t```python\n388\tentry = json.loads(line)\n389\tmsg = entry.get(\"message\", {})\n390\trole = msg.get(\"role\", \"\")     # \"user\" or \"assistant\"\n391\tcontent = msg.get(\"content\", \"\")  # string or list of content blocks\n392\t```\n393\t\n394\tContent can be a string or a list of blocks (`{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"...\"}` dicts).\n395\t\n396\t---\n397\t\n398\t## Script Details\n399\t\n400\t### compile.py - The Compiler\n401\t\n402\tUses the Claude Agent SDK's async streaming `query()`:\n403\t\n404\t```python\n405\tasync for message in query(\n406\t    prompt=compile_prompt,\n407\t    options=ClaudeAgentOptions(\n408\t        cwd=str(ROOT_DIR),\n409\t        system_prompt={\"type\": \"preset\", \"preset\": \"claude_code\"},\n410\t        allowed_tools=[\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Edit\", \"Glob\", \"Grep\"],\n411\t        permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\",\n412\t        max_turns=30,\n413\t    ),\n414\t):\n415\t```\n416\t\n417\t- Builds a prompt with: AGENTS.md schema, current index, all existing articles, and the daily log\n418\t- Claude reads the daily log, decides what concepts to extract, and writes files directly\n419\t- `permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\"` auto-approves all file operations\n420\t- Incremental: tracks SHA-256 hashes of daily logs in `state.json`, skips unchanged files\n421\t- Cost: ~$0.45-0.65 per daily log (increases as KB grows)\n422\t\n423\t**CLI:**\n424\t```bash\n425\tuv run python scripts/compile.py              # compile new/changed only\n426\tuv run python scripts/compile.py --all        # force recompile everything\n427\tuv run python scripts/compile.py --file daily/2026-04-01.md\n428\tuv run python scripts/compile.py --dry-run\n429\t```\n430\t\n431\t### query.py - Index-Guided Retrieval\n432\t\n433\tLoads the entire knowledge base into context (index + all articles). No RAG.\n434\t\n435\tAt personal KB scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms vector similarity. The LLM understands what you're really asking; cosine similarity just finds similar words.\n436\t\n437\t**CLI:**\n438\t```bash\n439\tuv run python scripts/query.py \"What auth patterns do I use?\"\n440\tuv run python scripts/query.py \"What's my error handling strategy?\" --file-back\n441\t```\n442\t\n443\tWith `--file-back`, creates a Q&A article in `knowledge/qa/` and updates the index and log. This is the compounding loop - every question makes the KB smarter.\n444\t\n445\t### lint.py - Health Checks\n446\t\n447\tSeven checks:\n448\t\n449\t| Check | Type | Catches |\n450\t|-------|------|---------|\n451\t| Broken links | Structural | `[[wikilinks]]` to non-existent articles |\n452\t| Orphan pages | Structural | Articles with zero inbound links |\n453\t| Orphan sources | Structural | Daily logs not yet compiled |\n454\t| Stale articles | Structural | Source logs changed since compilation |\n455\t| Missing backlinks | Structural | A links to B but B doesn't link back |\n456\t| Sparse articles | Structural | Under 200 words |\n457\t| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting claims across articles |\n458\t\n459\t**CLI:**\n460\t```bash\n461\tuv run python scripts/lint.py                    # all checks\n462\tuv run python scripts/lint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\n463\t```\n464\t\n465\tReports saved to `reports/lint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`.\n466\t\n467\t### memlint.py - Memory Tier Health Checks\n468\t\n469\t`lint.py` stops at `knowledge/`. The files the harness actually injects into every\n470\tsession live elsewhere and were never audited: `~/.claude/global-memory/` and the\n471\tactive `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/` slugs. Drift there is the most expensive\n472\tdrift in the system, because it is read at the start of every conversation.\n473\t\n474\tSeven checks:\n475\t\n476\t| Check | Type | Catches |\n477\t|-------|------|---------|\n478\t| Index drift | Structural | Memory file missing from MEMORY.md, or an index pointer with no file |\n479\t| Broken links | Structural | `[[links]]` that do not match any frontmatter `name:` in the slug |\n480\t| Name form | Structural | `name:` that is not the bare kebab stem (type belongs in `metadata.type`) |\n481\t| Near-duplicates | Structural | Within-slug TF-IDF similarity above the threshold |\n482\t| Cap overruns | Structural | A file past the character cap it declares in its own header |\n483\t| Content in index | Structural | Facts written into MEMORY.md, which is meant to hold pointers |\n484\t| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting, redundant, or superseded claims across memory files |\n485\t\n486\t**Scope.** Retired `C--Users-eric-rash*` slugs are excluded everywhere - the macOS\n487\tslugs were re-keyed from them, so including them reports history as duplication.\n488\t`handoffs/` and `transcripts/` are session records, not memory, and are never linted.\n489\tThe LLM check defaults to `global-memory` + the `DEV` and `~` slugs, the ones that\n490\tload together in a working session; `--all-slugs` widens it.\n491\t\n492\t**Similarity threshold.** Default 0.45. Measured 2026-09-01: the highest genuine\n493\twithin-slug pair scored 0.33. The report always prints the top pairs per slug so\n494\tthe number can be retuned against evidence.\n495\t\n496\t**Report only.** memlint never edits a memory file. Merging and deleting memories\n497\tstays a human decision.\n498\t\n499\t**CLI:**\n500\t```bash\n501\tuv run python scripts/memlint.py                    # all checks\n502\tuv run python scripts/memlint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\n503\tuv run python scripts/memlint.py --all-slugs        # widen the LLM check\n504\tuv run python scripts/memlint.py --threshold 0.6    # retune near-duplicates\n505\t```\n506\t\n507\tReports saved to `reports/memlint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`; `--structural-only` writes\n508\t`memlint-YYYY-MM-DD-structural.md` instead, so a free run never clobbers the\n509\tday's paid-for LLM findings. Runs automatically after the Sunday 02:30\n510\tadjudication job.\n511\t\n512\t---\n513\t\n514\t## State Tracking\n515\t\n516\t`scripts/state.json` tracks:\n517\t- `ingested` - map of daily log filenames to SHA-256 hashes, compilation timestamps, and costs\n518\t- `query_count` - total queries run\n519\t- `last_lint` - timestamp of most recent lint\n520\t- `last_memlint` - timestamp of most recent memory-tier lint\n521\t- `total_cost` - cumulative API cost\n522\t\n523\t`scripts/last-flush.json` tracks flush deduplication (session_id + timestamp).\n524\t\n525\tBoth are gitignored and regenerated automatically.\n526\t\n527\t---\n528\t\n529\t## Dependencies\n530\t\n531\t`pyproject.toml` (at project root):\n532\t- `claude-agent-sdk>=0.1.29` - Claude Agent SDK for LLM calls with tool use\n533\t- `python-dotenv>=1.0.0` - Environment variable management\n534\t- `tzdata>=2024.1` - Timezone data\n535\t- Python 3.12+, managed by [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)\n536\t\n537\tNo API key needed - uses Claude Code's built-in credentials at `~/.claude/.credentials.json`.\n538\t\n539\t---\n540\t\n541\t## Costs\n542\t\n543\t| Operation | Cost |\n544\t|-----------|------|\n545\t| Compile one daily log | $0.45-0.65 |\n546\t| Query (no file-back) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n547\t| Query (with file-back) | ~$0.25-0.40 |\n548\t| Full lint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n549\t| Structural lint only | $0.00 |\n550\t| Full memlint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n551\t| Structural memlint only | $0.00 |\n552\t| Memory flush (per session) | ~$0.02-0.05 |\n553\t\n554\t---\n555\t\n556\t## Customization\n557\t\n558\t### Additional Article Types\n559\t\n560\tAdd directories like `people/`, `projects/`, `tools/` to `knowledge/`. Define the article format in this file (AGENTS.md) and update `utils.py`'s `list_wiki_articles()` to include them.\n561\t\n562\t### Obsidian Integration\n563\t\n564\tThe knowledge base is pure markdown with `[[wikilinks]]` - works natively in Obsidian. Point a vault at `knowledge/` for graph view, backlinks, and search.\n565\t\n566\t### Scaling Beyond Index-Guided Retrieval\n567\t\n568\tAt ~2,000+ articles / ~2M+ tokens, the index becomes too large for the context window. At that point, add hybrid RAG (keyword + semantic search) as a retrieval layer before the LLM. See Karpathy's recommendation of `qmd` by Tobi Lutke for search at scale.\n569\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"972285da-8a0d-4458-8c9b-e31335054cdf","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
