{"parentUuid":"c6f29601-0a7d-4ced-ade2-f734c629064e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"session_context","context":{"userEmail":"The user's email address is eric.rash11@gmail.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks."}},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ebb6f7c9-3c64-495b-8fd3-7cce8c05fc9d","timestamp":"2026-10-01T11:08:53.633Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nAs you answer the user's questions, you can use the following context:\n# userEmail\nThe user's email address is eric.rash11@gmail.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.\n\nIMPORTANT: this context may or may not be relevant to your tasks. You should not respond to this context unless it is highly relevant to your task.\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"972285da-8a0d-4458-8c9b-e31335054cdf","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"ebb6f7c9-3c64-495b-8fd3-7cce8c05fc9d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"date","date":"2026-10-01"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"4f17a900-4bba-45d5-add0-96a1c00f981d","timestamp":"2026-10-01T11:08:53.633Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nToday's date is 2026-10-01.\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"972285da-8a0d-4458-8c9b-e31335054cdf","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"type":"last-prompt","lastPrompt":"You are a knowledge compiler. Your job is to read a daily conversation log and extract knowledge into structured wiki articles.  ## Schema (AGENTS.md)  # AGENTS.md - Personal Knowledge Base Schema  >…","leafUuid":"4f17a900-4bba-45d5-add0-96a1c00f981d","sessionId":"972285da-8a0d-4458-8c9b-e31335054cdf"}
{"type":"atis-latch","atis":"","sessionId":"972285da-8a0d-4458-8c9b-e31335054cdf"}
{"parentUuid":"4f17a900-4bba-45d5-add0-96a1c00f981d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfbQtaRmpsLSth4hwKVN6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"===ARTICLE concepts/memory-index-size-as-recurring-session-cost.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Project Memory Index Size as a Recurring Per-Session Cost\"\naliases: [project memory index trim, 8162 to 5549 chars, when-to-open line format, memory index grew to 8200 chars, index lines state when to open not what is inside, 56 links verified, doctor-plus context audit]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, maintenance, context-budget, macos, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-30.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-30\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# Project Memory Index Size as a Recurring Per-Session Cost\n\nOn 2026-09-30 a `doctor-plus` context audit found the project memory index had grown to roughly 8,200 characters — a cost paid at the start of every session, because the index is auto-loaded rather than retrieved on demand. The index was trimmed from 8,162 to 5,549 characters (~30% smaller) by rewriting each line to state only *when to open* the file it points at, leaving all dates, counts, and error text inside the memory files themselves. All 56 links were verified after the rewrite.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The index is always-loaded, so its size is a standing tax**, not a one-time storage cost; growth that would be unremarkable in a retrievable file is expensive here.\n- **The durable fix is a line-format rule, not a one-off cut:** each index line states only the condition under which a reader should open the target file. Details belong in the target.\n- **Trim measured 8,162 → 5,549 chars (~30%)** with no pointer removed — the reduction came entirely from moving content down a tier, not from dropping entries.\n- **All 56 links were verified after the rewrite**, because a reformatting pass over a pointer file is exactly the operation most likely to produce a dangling pointer.\n- **This is the same lever applied one layer out** from the Tier A index trim (15 → 10 files) and the repeated `MEMORY.md` cap trims, and it has the same failure mode: without a format rule, the file re-grows.\n\n## Details\n\nA pointer index occupies an unusual position in the memory architecture: it is the mechanism by which content becomes retrievable rather than resident, and yet it is itself resident. That makes it the one file where restating a fact is doubly expensive — the fact is paid for in the index every session *and* stored again in the file the index points at. The observed growth to ~8,200 characters came from exactly that duplication: index lines had accumulated dates, counts, and error strings that already existed in their targets, apparently on the reasoning that a richer index saves a read. The trim rejects that trade, because a read is conditional and cheap while the index is unconditional.\n\nEncoding the fix as a line *format* rather than a size target is what distinguishes this pass from the earlier wording-compression attempts on `MEMORY.md` catalogued in [[concepts/memory-cap-relief-by-migration-not-deletion]]. Compression shortens prose and buys bytes that are immediately re-spent; a format rule (\"state when to open this file, nothing else\") answers the question that produces the growth in the first place, so the next person adding an entry knows what not to write. It also makes the index auditable: a line containing a date or a count is visibly out of format and can be fixed without a judgment call about whether it is worth its bytes. This is the same move as recording a mechanism instead of a magic number in [[connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number]] — the abstraction level of the recorded content is chosen to match how stable that content is.\n\nThe link verification step matters because a bulk reformat is precisely the operation that silently breaks pointers. Fifty-six links rewritten by hand or by pattern is fifty-six chances to mistype a target, and a broken index entry is worse than a verbose one: a verbose entry costs tokens, while a dangling one resolves to nothing and makes a real memory file invisible. Verifying after the pass rather than trusting the edit is the same discipline as re-running the doctor to confirm a cap fix, and the same hazard as the stale-pointer class described in [[connections/a-stale-pointer-resolves-cleanly-to-the-wrong-answer]].\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/memory-cap-relief-by-migration-not-deletion]] — The adjacent file under the same pressure, where wording compression repeatedly failed and migration worked; this trim is the migration logic applied to the index itself.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The tier structure that makes \"resident vs retrievable\" the operative distinction.\n- [[concepts/system-health-check-remediation]] — The Tier A auto-injection index trimmed 15 → 10 files for the same reason, with the same \"stays on disk, stays searchable\" guarantee.\n- [[concepts/session-context-footprint-audit]] — The audit genre that measures what is always loaded; this run is an instance of it.\n- [[connections/always-loaded-context-is-the-expensive-place-to-be-wrong]] — Volume as one of three costs on the session-resident surface.\n- [[connections/keep-it-retrievable-not-resident]] — The governing principle: cut what is always loaded, never what is retrievable.\n- [[connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number]] — Recording content at the abstraction level that matches its stability.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-30.md]] — End-of-day upkeep session (12:33): doctor-plus context audit finding the project memory index at ~8,200 chars with all four 8/29 problems fixed; index trimmed 8,162 → 5,549 chars (~30%) with each line rewritten to state only when to open a file and details left in the memory files; all 56 links verified.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/stranded-flush-file-archive-not-recover.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Archiving a Stranded Session-Flush File Rather Than Recovering It\"\naliases: [stranded memory-save file, 9/21 session flush stranded, _stranded-archive, recovery would date its facts as today, 1 of 12 stranded files left, Mac-local never git-tracked]\ntags: [claude-code, memory, maintenance, provenance, macos, workflow]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-30.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-30\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# Archiving a Stranded Session-Flush File Rather Than Recovering It\n\nA `doctor-plus` audit on 2026-09-30 found one remaining stranded session-flush file (dated 9/21) out of twelve originally flagged on 8/29. Rather than ingest its contents into the memory tier, it was archived to `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/scripts/_stranded-archive/` and kept Mac-local, never git-tracked. The stated reason was temporal provenance: the file was nine days old, and recovering it would have stamped its facts with today's date.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Recovery was rejected on provenance grounds, not value grounds.** A flush file ingested nine days late would enter the memory tier dated as current, misrepresenting when each fact was true.\n- **Archive, do not delete.** The file was moved to a dedicated `_stranded-archive/` directory, so the decision is reversible if the content later turns out to matter.\n- **The archive is deliberately Mac-local and untracked**, keeping a one-off salvage artifact out of the repository's history rather than committing debris.\n- **Stranded files drained from 12 to 1 over roughly a month**, so the backlog was largely self-clearing and the remaining file was a residual rather than a symptom of an active fault.\n- **The user chose both offered remedies** (\"archive it and trim the index\"), pairing this disposal with the memory-index trim in the same pass.\n\n## Details\n\nThe provenance argument is the transferable part of this decision. A session-flush file is a time-stamped record of what was true during one session; its value depends on the reader knowing *when*. Memory-tier ingestion at a later date attaches the ingestion moment rather than the origin moment, and a fact about the stack as it stood on 9/21 presented as current is not merely stale but actively misleading — the same hazard as a stale pointer resolving cleanly to the wrong answer in [[connections/a-stale-pointer-resolves-cleanly-to-the-wrong-answer]]. Nine days is long enough in this stack for backup job counts, error-log contents, and scheduled-task state to have all turned over, so the file's claims could not be trusted as written and re-verifying each one would cost more than the content was worth.\n\nArchiving rather than deleting preserves optionality at near-zero cost, the same reversibility instinct that governed moving rather than deleting 98 skills during a prune and taking tarball snapshots before destructive git operations. The additional choice to keep the archive Mac-local and untracked draws a line that the broader stack applies inconsistently: a salvage artifact is not a project asset, and committing it would permanently enlarge a repository's history with content whose whole premise is that it is probably not needed. That is the opposite disposition from a handoff document, which exists to be read by a future session and therefore must live somewhere durable and discoverable — compare the recurring temp-path placement failures in [[concepts/handoff-document-placement-discipline]] and [[concepts/scratch-folder-artifact-durability]]. The distinguishing question is whether anyone is expected to look for the file: handoffs are, archives are not.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/memory-index-size-as-recurring-session-cost]] — The other half of the same remediation pass, chosen by the user in the same breath.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — The compiler whose flush pipeline produced the stranded file.\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — The same archive-then-clear procedure applied to the escalation log, including the requirement to confirm staleness first.\n- [[concepts/session-context-footprint-audit]] — The audit that enumerated the original twelve stranded files.\n- [[concepts/handoff-document-placement-discipline]] — The contrasting case: a file meant to be found must not be put somewhere disposable.\n- [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] — Why a safety copy is only useful if its location and naming actually survive.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-30.md]] — End-of-day upkeep session (12:33): one of twelve stranded memory-save files remaining; the 9/21 session-flush file archived to `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/scripts/_stranded-archive/` rather than recovered because it was nine days old and recovery would date its facts as today; archive kept Mac-local and never git-tracked.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/repo-behind-blocks-nightly-push.md===\n---\ntitle: \"A Repo Behind GitHub Silently Blocks the Nightly Backup\"\naliases: [repo-behind-blocked:global-memory, backup never auto-merges, pull then re-run backup, 7:51 backup failed global-memory behind, persistent vs transient push failure, repo out of sync blocks backup job]\ntags: [automation, backup, git, macos, launchd, maintenance, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-24.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-30.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-24\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# A Repo Behind GitHub Silently Blocks the Nightly Backup\n\nThe daily backup job refuses to push a repository that is behind its remote, because it never auto-merges. On 2026-09-24 this surfaced as `repo-behind-blocked:global-memory` failing two nights in a row; pulling by hand and re-running produced a clean 21:12 pass. The same mechanism recurred on 2026-09-30, where the 07:51 backup failure was traced to `global-memory` being behind GitHub — recorded there in its general form: a repo out of sync silently blocks the backup job.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The backup will not merge on your behalf**, by design — an automated job that resolves divergence unsupervised can lose work, so it stops and reports instead.\n- **The failure is persistent, not transient.** It recurs every night until a human pulls, which distinguishes it from a network-flake push failure that self-heals on the next run.\n- **The remedy is `pull`, then a verified re-run** — not a retry and not a force. A fix is not its own confirmation; the clean 21:12 run is what closed the 9/24 instance.\n- **One lagging repo fails the whole job's status**, so the error says less about backup coverage than its severity suggests; the backed-up content is usually fine and only the push leg is stuck.\n- **By 2026-09-30 the condition was general knowledge about the job**, stated without reference to a particular repo — evidence it had been seen often enough to generalize.\n\n## Details\n\nThe triage that matters here is persistent-versus-transient. Push failures in this stack come in two shapes with opposite correct responses: a network or credential hiccup resolves itself on the next scheduled run and should simply be observed, while a behind-remote block is a structural state that no number of retries will change. Reading the second as the first produces the worst outcome available — an error that appears nightly, is assumed self-healing, and trains the operator to skim a channel that also carries real coverage gaps. That is the same degradation described in [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] for a permanently-firing manifest warning, and the reason [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] insists on a verified clean pass rather than an intended fix before clearing the log.\n\nThe refusal itself is correct and worth defending. A backup job running unattended at 07:51 or 21:00 has no way to adjudicate a divergence, and `global-memory` is exactly the repository where an automatic merge could destroy content — it is written by two machines, and its transcript logs are append-only records whose correct merge is a union rather than a winner, as established in [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]]. Stopping with a named error hands the decision to the only party able to make it. The cost is that the job's aggregate status goes red for a reason unrelated to whether files were copied, which is the recurring scope mismatch catalogued in [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]]: the status field is locally truthful while answering a narrower question than the operator is asking.\n\nPractically, the condition argues for pulling `global-memory` before the evening backup window rather than discovering the block the next morning. The 2026-09-30 session took the complementary route — committing and pushing immediately rather than waiting on the 21:00 job, at the user's request — which removes the window in which the repo can drift behind in the first place.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/mac-evening-machine-schedule-split]] — The 21:00 backup slot whose clean run closed the 9/24 instance.\n- [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] — Why the error stays until a verified pass, not until the pull is done.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] — Why `global-memory` in particular must not be auto-merged.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The companion reading problem: an `OK` status that does not describe coverage.\n- [[concepts/git-sync-status-versus-uncommitted-work]] — Deferring a commit to the nightly job is what creates the drift window this error reports.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The general shape of a truthful status answering the wrong question.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-pass-2026-09-30]] — The 2026-09-30 pass where this cause was identified and stated generally.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-24.md]] — `repo-behind-blocked:global-memory` failing two nights because the backup never auto-merges; pull followed by a clean 21:12 run; persistent-vs-transient push-failure triage.\n- [[daily/2026-09-30.md]] — End-of-day upkeep session (12:33): the 07:51 backup failure traced to `global-memory` being behind GitHub, recorded in general form as \"a repo out of sync silently blocks the backup job\"; commits pushed immediately rather than waiting for the 21:00 job.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/stack-doctor-pass-2026-09-30.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Stack Doctor Pass (2026-09-30): All Five Jobs OK and Two Lagging Warnings\"\naliases: [2026-09-30 stack doctor, 5 scheduled jobs OK, empty error log, both machines in step, flush-error will age off, backup-error clears tomorrow, improvement over 9/28]\ntags: [claude-code, automation, maintenance, backup, macos, memory, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-30.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-30\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# Stack Doctor Pass (2026-09-30): All Five Jobs OK and Two Lagging Warnings\n\nA `/claude-stack-doctor` run on 2026-09-30 reported all five scheduled jobs OK, an empty error log, and both machines in step with GitHub — a marked improvement over 2026-09-28, when the backup job was failing and five errors were outstanding. The two residual warnings were both lagging indicators rather than live faults: a `backup-error` entry awaiting confirmation from the next 21:00 run, and a `flush-error` from 9/27 (a usage limit) expected to age off unaided.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Verdict: 5/5 scheduled jobs OK, error log empty, both machines synced** — against a 9/28 baseline of a failing backup job and five errors.\n- **The 07:51 backup failure's cause was identified, not merely cleared:** `global-memory` was behind GitHub, which blocks the push leg because the job never auto-merges.\n- **Both remaining warnings were classified as lagging, with their clearing conditions named:** the `backup-error` entry clears when the 21:00 run is confirmed clean, and the 9/27 `flush-error` (usage limit) ages off on its own.\n- **Two remediations were performed in one pass** — archiving a stranded 9/21 flush file and trimming the project memory index 8,162 → 5,549 chars — after the user chose both offered fixes.\n- **Commits were pushed immediately rather than deferred to the 21:00 backup**, at the user's request, closing the drift window that caused the morning failure.\n\n## Details\n\nThe pass's distinguishing feature is that every remaining warning was given a clearing condition rather than being either fixed or ignored. A `backup-error` entry whose underlying cause has been addressed is not yet evidence of health, because the fix has not been exercised; the correct disposition is to name the event that will confirm it — here the next 21:00 run — and leave the entry standing until then. The 9/27 `flush-error` was treated differently and correctly: a usage-limit failure is transient by construction, so it needs no intervention and its persistence in the log is purely a function of retention. Separating \"fixed, awaiting proof\" from \"self-resolving\" from \"live\" is what keeps the escalation channel meaningful, and it is the discipline that [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] and [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] both formalize from opposite directions.\n\nComparing against a prior dated baseline is the second useful habit on display. \"All five jobs OK\" is unremarkable in isolation but informative against 9/28's failing backup and five errors, because the delta identifies which interventions landed. The same comparative reading applied to the context audit, where all four problems recorded on 8/29 were confirmed fixed and exactly one of twelve stranded memory-save files remained — a backlog that had largely self-drained. This mirrors the post-restart ALL CLEAR framing in [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] and the 8 → 6 accounting in [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]], where the number only carries meaning relative to the previous sweep.\n\nThe closeout leg surfaced work belonging to other sessions: a full-`DEV` sweep found `DEV/CommandCentre` dirty with `server.out` and `DEV/graphify-out` dirty with graph snapshots, both generated artifacts rather than authored content. Committing them (`3cae9d2`, `d4d1b5f`) resolves the immediate dirtiness but is the per-artifact remedy; the structural reading from [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] is that a repository routinely dirtied by its own output should ignore that output instead. One item was deliberately left uncommitted with the decision deferred: `~/.claude/skills/.trash/` and its three deleted files.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The health-check routine this pass instantiates, including the `.errors.log` escalation channel.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — An earlier sweep scored the same way, against its own prior baseline.\n- [[concepts/repo-behind-blocks-nightly-push]] — The diagnosed cause of the 07:51 backup failure.\n- [[concepts/memory-index-size-as-recurring-session-cost]] — One of the two remediations chosen in this pass.\n- [[concepts/stranded-flush-file-archive-not-recover]] — The other remediation, and its provenance rationale.\n- [[concepts/session-context-footprint-audit]] — The doctor-plus context audit whose 8/29 findings were confirmed fixed here.\n- [[concepts/closeout-scope-discipline-other-sessions-work]] — The scope question raised by finding other sessions' dirty repos during a closeout sweep.\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — Why lagging entries are left standing rather than cleared on intent.\n- [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — The reading that `server.out` and graph snapshots should be ignored rather than committed.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-30.md]] — End-of-day upkeep and `/closeout` session (12:33): stack doctor showing all 5 scheduled jobs OK, empty error log, both machines in step with GitHub, against a 9/28 baseline of a failing backup and 5 errors; doctor-plus audit confirming all four 8/29 problems fixed with 1 of 12 stranded memory-save files left; 07:51 backup failure traced to `global-memory` behind GitHub; index trim and stranded-file archive both performed; closeout sweep finding `DEV/CommandCentre` (`server.out`) and `DEV/graphify-out` (graph snapshots) dirty from other sessions; `~/.claude/skills/.trash/` left uncommitted on purpose; commits `ebfb874`, `bb3ce1b`, `4dc349d`, `a2f4f78`, `3cae9d2`, `d4d1b5f`; report at `~/.claude/skills/doctor-plus/findings-2026-09-30.md`.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE connections/name-the-clearing-condition-for-every-residual-warning.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Name the Clearing Condition for Every Residual Warning\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/stack-doctor-pass-2026-09-30\"\n  - \"concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run\"\n  - \"concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-30.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-30\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# Connection: Name the Clearing Condition for Every Residual Warning\n\n## The Connection\n\nA maintenance pass that ends with warnings still showing has three available dispositions, and the stack has independently arrived at all three. On 2026-09-30 the two residual stack-doctor warnings were each assigned one explicitly: the `backup-error` entry was *fixed but unproven*, clearing when the next 21:00 run is confirmed clean; the 9/27 `flush-error` was *self-resolving*, a usage-limit failure that ages off unaided. On 2026-09-15 `.errors.log` was archived and cleared only after a verified 0-error / 86-job re-run — the \"fixed but unproven\" case held open until proof arrived. On 2026-08-31 the entries were *stale*, confirmed resolved against the backup log's timeline, then archived and cleared.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nThe three dispositions share a structure that is easy to miss when the warnings are simply listed as \"remaining issues\": each one is a claim about a *future event*, and the claim is the useful part. \"Fixed but unproven\" names a specific run that will settle it. \"Self-resolving\" names retention as the only mechanism needed. \"Stale\" names the independent source that already settled it. A warning left in the log without one of these three labels is indistinguishable from a live fault, and that indistinguishability is what eventually makes the whole channel unreadable — the operator cannot tell which entries are awaiting an event, which are awaiting nothing, and which are awaiting them.\n\nThe non-obvious consequence is that classification must happen at the moment of the fix, not at the next pass. The person who just pulled `global-memory` is the only one who knows that the 21:00 run is the discriminating event; a week later the entry is just an old error, and the honest options collapse to re-investigating or clearing on a guess. This is why the 9/30 session's phrasing — \"tomorrow: confirm the 21:00 backup ran clean — that clears the last warning\" — is doing real work: it converts a residual warning into a scheduled check with a named pass condition, which is the same conversion that distinguishes a verified clean re-run from an intended fix. It also explains the asymmetry between the two 9/30 warnings: the usage-limit flush error needed no check because its clearing condition requires no action, so recording that fact is what prevents a future session from investigating it.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-09-30 (12:33): stack doctor left two warnings, each classified — the `backup-error` entry to be cleared by confirming the 21:00 backup ran clean, and the 9/27 `flush-error` (usage limit) expected to \"age off on its own.\"\n- 2026-09-15: `.errors.log` archived and cleared only after a verified 0-error / 86-job re-run, on the stated principle that fixes are not their own confirmation.\n- 2026-08-31 (10:34): `.errors.log` entries confirmed stale against the backup log's timeline — the 09:00 manifest failure had a 10:31 clean re-run — then archived before clearing.\n- 2026-08-11: the stack-doctor sweep's 8 → 6 verdict explicitly noted three of the six remaining issues as lagging indicators that clear on the next nightly backup, and recorded reasons for every deliberately-deferred item.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-pass-2026-09-30]]\n- [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]]\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]]\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — The earlier sweep that separated lagging indicators from live faults and recorded reasons for deferrals.\n- [[concepts/repo-behind-blocks-nightly-push]] — The persistent-vs-transient triage that decides which disposition a push failure gets.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The escalation channel whose readability depends on this classification.\n- [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — Why an unlabelled permanent entry degrades the channel that reported it.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/memory-index-size-as-recurring-session-cost]] | Project memory index trimmed 8,162→5,549 chars by rewriting each line to state only when to open a file; always-loaded so size is a standing tax, 56 links verified | 2026-09-30.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[concepts/stranded-flush-file-archive-not-recover]] | A 9-day-old stranded flush file archived rather than recovered because ingestion would date its facts as today; archive kept Mac-local and untracked | 2026-09-30.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[concepts/repo-behind-blocks-nightly-push]] | Adds the 2026-09-30 recurrence stating the cause in general form: a repo out of sync silently blocks the backup job, fixed by pull then a verified re-run | 2026-09-24.md, 2026-09-30.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[concepts/stack-doctor-pass-2026-09-30]] | 5/5 jobs OK and empty error log against a 9/28 baseline of a failing backup and 5 errors; two residual warnings classified as lagging with named clearing conditions | 2026-09-30.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[connections/name-the-clearing-condition-for-every-residual-warning]] | Fixed-but-unproven, self-resolving, and stale are the three dispositions a residual warning can get, and the label must be applied at the moment of the fix | 2026-09-30.md | 2026-10-01 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-10-01T06:08:52-05:00] compile | 2026-09-30.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-30.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/memory-index-size-as-recurring-session-cost]], [[concepts/stranded-flush-file-archive-not-recover]], [[concepts/stack-doctor-pass-2026-09-30]], [[connections/name-the-clearing-condition-for-every-residual-warning]]\n- Articles updated: [[concepts/repo-behind-blocks-nightly-push]]\n===END LOG==="}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":94403,"cache_read_input_tokens":9707,"output_tokens":10182,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":94403,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10182,"cache_read_input_tokens":9707,"cache_creation_input_tokens":94403,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":94403},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfbQtZxFcYR2HBG9ZPFVF","type":"assistant","uuid":"fcd650bc-c259-4c6f-9d7e-734625ae2b9c","timestamp":"2026-10-01T11:11:04.703Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"972285da-8a0d-4458-8c9b-e31335054cdf","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
